er-client-data-experimentation\",\"children\":\"{\\\"status\\\":\\\"uninitialized\\\"}\"}]]}]}]\n"])</script><script nonce="YjU4NmQyOTUtZjdmZS00N2I5LWI3OTYtOGRhNmNlNGE0YWFj">self.__next_f.push([1,"e:[[\"$\",\"meta\",\"0\",{\"charSet\":\"utf-8\"}],[\"$\",\"meta\",\"1\",{\"name\":\"viewport\",\"content\":\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"}]]\na:null\n"])</script><script nonce="YjU4NmQyOTUtZjdmZS00N2I5LWI3OTYtOGRhNmNlNGE0YWFj">self.__next_f.push([1,"1f:I[42712,[\"8739\",\"static/chunks/63f0ec43-8ee6a76d70472249.js\",\"2254\",\"static/chunks/8208b75a-a48e5a4507a0de91.js\",\"4684\",\"static/chunks/24cf1b50-159cca90b09285e0.js\",\"1829\",\"static/chunks/9ffa21ba-c069766d809bd4c7.js\",\"7670\",\"static/chunks/7670-7326298c9856172b.js\",\"282\",\"static/chunks/282-691fffb366e24ca5.js\",\"7515\",\"static/chunks/7515-336759ef5dad2b52.js\",\"5497\",\"static/chunks/5497-ab85ef3ea59dd353.js\",\"7720\",\"static/chunks/7720-3a1e7e411adba2d3.js\",\"6281\",\"static/chunks/6281-c6e84786dade3614.js\",\"7086\",\"static/chunks/7086-1f1c4891d766e04f.js\",\"600\",\"static/chunks/600-9a9fcafaaa6c1c4c.js\",\"5002\",\"static/chunks/5002-9735c25beda556ba.js\",\"5448\",\"static/chunks/5448-2a3cfd853d899c9a.js\",\"5855\",\"static/chunks/5855-a6c0fea05a2e12ad.js\",\"4393\",\"static/chunks/4393-8a68969d02bf05c7.js\",\"9214\",\"static/chunks/9214-a10614844a67ba31.js\",\"6049\",\"static/chunks/6049-cddc8492d6c32af0.js\",\"4341\",\"static/chunks/4341-6051b2037c635ccb.js\",\"6304\",\"static/chunks/app/page/%5Bslug%5D/page-cf436f13a3485a2d.js\"],\"HydrationBoundary\"]\n"])</script><script nonce="YjU4NmQyOTUtZjdmZS00N2I5LWI3OTYtOGRhNmNlNGE0YWFj">self.__next_f.push([1,"22:I[6367,[],\"IconMark\"]\n20:T1ec02,"])</script><script nonce="YjU4NmQyOTUtZjdmZS00N2I5LWI3OTYtOGRhNmNlNGE0YWFj">self.__next_f.push([1,"# Middle Ages\n\n![Europe at the onset of the Middle Ages](./_assets_/Europe_and_the_Near_East_at_476_AD.png)\n\nThe Middle Ages, spanning approximately from the 5th to the 15th century in Europe, followed the collapse of centralized Roman authority and preceded the widespread adoption of Renaissance humanism and early modern state formation.[](https://dcc.newberry.org/?p=14383)[](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5062728/) This millennium-long era witnessed the transition from late antiquity's urban-oriented civilization to a predominantly agrarian, decentralized society structured around localized lord-vassal relationships and manorial estates, with the Catholic Church emerging as the primary unifying institution amid political fragmentation.[](http://webspace.ship.edu/cgboer/middleages.html)[](https://pressbooks.cuny.edu/thebirthofeurope/chapter/chapter-13-early-medieval-europe/) Key subdivisions include the Early Middle Ages (c. 500–1000), defined by Germanic migrations, the establishment of successor kingdoms, and economic contraction; the High Middle Ages (c. 1000–1300), featuring demographic expansion, agricultural innovations like the three-field system, the founding of universities, and Gothic architectural achievements; and the Late Middle Ages (c. 1300–1500), marked by crises such as the Black Death, the Hundred Years' War, and the Avignon Papacy, yet also by proto-capitalist trade networks and intellectual shifts toward nominalism and conciliarism.[](https://yalebooks.yale.edu/2020/08/18/the-high-middle-ages/) Despite 19th-century historiographical labels like \"Dark Ages\" emphasizing cultural stagnation—often rooted in Enlightenment biases against feudal and ecclesiastical dominance—empirical evidence reveals substantial continuity in legal traditions, technological adaptations (e.g., heavy plow and horse collar), and monastic preservation of classical texts, countering narratives of wholesale regression.[](https://salutemmundo.wordpress.com/2021/10/14/thinking-about-the-dark-ages/)[](https://scholarworks.wmich.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1021\u0026context=english_pubs) Notable accomplishments encompassed the Carolingian Renaissance's scriptoria advancements, the scholastic synthesis of Aristotelian philosophy with Christian theology by figures like Thomas Aquinas, and the Crusades' stimulation of intercontinental exchange, which collectively fostered the institutional and intellectual frameworks underpinning Western Europe's eventual global ascendancy.[](https://yalebooks.yale.edu/2020/08/18/the-high-middle-ages/) Controversies persist in assessing the era's violence—evident in endemic feudal warfare and inquisitorial persecutions—against its causal role in evolving representative governance, as seen in the Magna Carta's limitation of monarchical power, underscoring the period's complex interplay of constraint and innovation rather than simplistic decline.[](http://webspace.ship.edu/cgboer/middleages.html)\n\n## Terminology and Periodisation\n\n### Origins and Evolution of the Term\n\nThe concept of an intermediate historical period between classical antiquity and a perceived cultural revival originated in the 14th century with the Italian scholar Francesco Petrarca (Petrarch), who characterized the era following the fall of the Western Roman Empire as a \"Dark Age\" marked by the decline of ancient learning and the rise of barbarism.[](https://www.medievalists.net/2023/06/middle-ages-dark-ages/)[](https://www.historyextra.com/period/medieval/how-when-why-did-we-start-calling-medieval-era-name/) Petrarch's critique, rooted in his admiration for Greco-Roman texts, positioned his own time as a nascent rebirth, implicitly framing the intervening centuries as a temporal bridge of obscurity rather than continuity.[](https://www.historyhit.com/why-were-the-early-middle-ages-called-the-dark-ages/)\n\nThis tripartite schema—ancient, middle, and modern—gained structure in the early 15th century through Renaissance humanists. Leonardo Bruni, in his historiographical works around 1400–1440, explicitly divided history into these phases, with the \"middle\" era commencing after the Roman Empire's decline and ending with contemporary renewal.[](https://www.historyextra.com/period/medieval/how-when-why-did-we-start-calling-medieval-era-name/) Flavio Biondo further formalized it in his *Historiarum ab inclinatione Romanorum imperii decades* (published 1439–1453), chronicling events from the 5th century sack of Rome to the 15th century as a distinct intermediary epoch of transformation, distinct from antiquity's grandeur and modernity's promise.[](https://www.britannica.com/biography/Flavio-Biondo)[](https://www.italyonthisday.com/2020/06/flavio-biondo-15th-century-historian-archaeologist.html) These scholars' periodization reflected a causal view of historical rupture caused by invasions and institutional decay, yet emphasized Europe's enduring Latin Christian inheritance over outright barbarity.[](https://www.britannica.com/topic/history-of-Europe/The-Middle-Ages)\n\nThe specific Latin phrase *medium aevum* (\"middle age\") first appeared in 1604, coined by the German jurist and historian Melchior Goldast in his legal and historical compilations, providing a neutral chronological label for the post-antique interval.[](https://history.stackexchange.com/questions/90/where-did-the-term-middle-ages-come-from) By the late 17th century, *medium aevum* had become standard in European scholarship for denoting this era, translating into vernacular equivalents like the English \"Middle Ages\" by the early 18th century.[](https://www.britannica.com/topic/history-of-Europe/The-Middle-Ages) The adjective \"medieval,\" an Anglicization of *medium aevum*, entered usage in the 19th century amid professional historiography's expansion, coinciding with reevaluations that tempered earlier \"Dark Ages\" disdain—evident in works by scholars like Edward Gibbon—by highlighting feudal innovations, Gothic architecture, and scholastic philosophy as causal foundations for later developments.[](https://www.thoughtco.com/definition-of-medieval-1789185) This evolution underscores the term's retrospective imposition, shaped by Enlightenment and Romantic biases privileging progress narratives, though it persists due to its utility in delineating empirical shifts in governance, economy, and culture from circa 500 to 1500 CE.[](https://www.thoughtco.com/definition-of-medieval-1789185)[](https://www.britannica.com/topic/history-of-Europe/The-Middle-Ages-in-modern-historiography)\n\n### Chronological Boundaries\n\nThe Middle Ages are conventionally delimited from the deposition of Romulus Augustulus, the last Western Roman emperor, by the Germanic chieftain Odoacer in 476 CE to the fall of Constantinople in 1453 CE.[](https://roman-emperors.sites.luc.edu/auggiero.htm)[](https://www.unrv.com/emperors/romulus-augustulus.php) This starting boundary reflects the effective collapse of centralized Roman administration in Western Europe, as Odoacer's forces captured the emperor's father Orestes on August 28, 476, and deposed the young Romulus shortly thereafter, around early September, establishing a new barbarian kingdom in Italy while nominally subordinating it to the Eastern Roman emperor Zeno.[](https://roman-emperors.sites.luc.edu/auggiero.htm)[](https://www.americanstandardgold.com/blog/the-day-the-western-roman-empire-ended.cfm) The endpoint of 1453 marks the Ottoman conquest of the Byzantine capital on May 29, extinguishing the final institutional continuity with the ancient Roman Empire and accelerating the influx of Greek scholars and classical texts into Western Europe.[](https://hum54-15.omeka.fas.harvard.edu/exhibits/show/theodosian_walls_gardens/fall)[](https://www.medievalists.net/2024/04/the-siege-of-constantinople-1453/)\n\nAlternative terminal dates include 1492 CE, tied to Christopher Columbus's voyage to the Americas and the fall of Granada completing the Christian Reconquista of Iberia, or broader late-15th-century markers like the widespread adoption of printing presses following Johannes Gutenberg's innovations around 1450.[](https://www.history.com/topics/middle-ages) These variations underscore that periodization prioritizes symbolic political ruptures over uniform cultural or economic shifts, with the 476–1453 span encompassing roughly a millennium of fragmented polities, feudal structures, and gradual institutional evolution in Europe.[](https://www.smarthistory.org/introduction-to-the-middle-ages/)\n\nHistoriographical conventions often approximate the era as spanning the 5th to 15th centuries CE, acknowledging regional disparities—such as earlier transitions in the British Isles post-410 Roman withdrawal or prolonged Byzantine and Islamic influences—but rejecting rigid caesurae due to evidentiary continuity in law, agriculture, and Christianity from late antiquity.[](https://dcc.newberry.org/?p=14383)[](https://irl.umsl.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1448\u0026context=thesis) Scholarly sources emphasize that such boundaries serve analytical purposes rather than denoting absolute discontinuities, as archaeological and textual evidence reveals persistent Roman-era practices amid 5th-century upheavals and incremental 15th-century innovations like gunpowder warfare and oceanic exploration.[](https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/pdf/10.2307/2846695)[](https://ejournals.bc.edu/index.php/orac/article/download/13673/10847/)\n\n### Historiographical Debates on Periodization\n\nThe term \"Middle Ages\" originated in the Italian Renaissance, with Petrarch in the mid-14th century describing the period after classical antiquity as one of cultural decline, positioning his era as a rebirth (*renovatio*).[](https://trmarvin.org/when-were-the-middle-ages-periodization-and-why-it-matters/) This pejorative framing, later formalized by Flavio Biondo in his *Historiarum ab inclinatio romani imperii decades* (1439–1453), cast the intervening millennium as a temporal bridge between ancient glory and modern revival, influencing subsequent historiography.[](https://trmarvin.org/when-were-the-middle-ages-periodization-and-why-it-matters/) By the 17th century, Christophorus Cellarius divided it into *antiquior* (early, to c. 1000), *media* (high, 1000–1300), and *recentior* (late, to 1453 or 1500), a schema persisting in textbooks despite critiques of its rigidity.[](https://trmarvin.org/when-were-the-middle-ages-periodization-and-why-it-matters/)\n\nDebates on chronological boundaries center on the starting point, traditionally fixed at 476 CE with the deposition of Romulus Augustulus, marking the Western Roman Empire's collapse amid barbarian invasions and internal decay.[](https://www.medievalists.net/2012/08/when-were-the-middle-ages-2/) However, scholars like those advocating a \"Late Antiquity\" extension to c. 800 CE argue for continuity in institutions, such as the Byzantine East and Merovingian adaptations of Roman administration, minimizing rupture in favor of gradual transformation.[](https://scholar.harvard.edu/files/puett/files/davis_puett_medieval_globe.pdf) Empirical evidence counters this softening: Western Europe's urban centers shrank dramatically—Rome's population fell from over 1 million in 100 CE to perhaps 20,000 by 700 CE—while literacy rates plummeted outside clerical circles, reflecting causal disruptions from invasions, economic fragmentation, and loss of centralized infrastructure rather than seamless evolution.[](https://www.oerproject.com/-/media/WHP/PDF/Era4/WHP-4-3-2-Read---The-Dark-Ages-Debate---610L.pdf) Alternative starts, such as Muhammad's Hijra in 622 CE or Charlemagne's coronation in 800 CE, incorporate Islamic expansions or Carolingian revival but risk Eurocentric imposition on non-Western timelines.[](https://www.medievalists.net/2012/08/when-were-the-middle-ages-2/)\n\nEnding dates provoke similar contention, with 1453 (fall of Constantinople) symbolizing the Ottoman closure of classical texts' Greek sources, or 1492 marking transatlantic exploration's economic shift.[](https://irl.umsl.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1448\u0026context=thesis) Jacques Le Goff proposed a \"long Middle Ages\" extending to the French Revolution (1789), emphasizing persistent feudal structures over abrupt Renaissance innovation.[](https://laviedesidees.fr/Pieces-of-History) Critiques highlight periodization's artificiality: it derives from 19th-century nationalist historiography, projecting modern ideological divides onto the past and obscuring regional variations, such as Byzantine endurance or Islamic golden ages.[](https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/01916599.2024.2373542) Yet, causal realism underscores real transitions—e.g., the 14th-century Black Death (killing 30–60% of Europe's population) and gunpowder warfare eroded feudalism—validating boundaries tied to verifiable demographic and technological shifts over purely narrative constructs.[](https://irl.umsl.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1448\u0026context=thesis) Modern \"global Middle Ages\" frameworks challenge Eurocentrism by synchronizing with Asian or African chronologies but often dilute empirical focus on Europe's post-Roman reconfiguration.[](https://scholar.harvard.edu/files/puett/files/davis_puett_medieval_globe.pdf)\n\nThe \"Dark Ages\" label, evoking Petrarchan disdain, faces rejection in academia for overstating barbarism amid monastic preservation of texts, yet data on halved life expectancies and abandoned aqueducts affirm a substantive regression in material culture until c. 1000 CE.[](https://www.oerproject.com/-/media/WHP/PDF/Era4/WHP-4-3-2-Read---The-Dark-Ages-Debate---610L.pdf) Historiographical bias creeps in: post-1960s scholarship, influenced by continuity paradigms, downplays these metrics to counter Enlightenment teleology, but primary sources like Gregory of Tours' chronicles depict vivid chaos from 5th–8th-century invasions.[](https://fiveable.me/the-middle-ages/unit-1/periodization-historiography-middle-ages/study-guide/ujv1onxdMXfhsMzZ) Ultimately, periodization aids synthesis but falters as absolute; debates persist because no single date encapsulates multifaceted causal chains, from climatic shifts to ideological realignments.[](https://going-medieval.com/2024/02/26/let-me-explain-something-to-you-periodisation-and-the-middle-ages/)\n\n## Transition from Late Antiquity (c. 300–600)\n\n### Internal Crises of the Roman Empire\n\nThe Crisis of the Third Century, spanning approximately 235 to 284 CE, marked a profound internal upheaval in the Roman Empire, characterized by rapid turnover of emperors—over 20 in less than 50 years—amid frequent assassinations, usurpations, and civil wars that eroded central authority.[](https://www.worldhistory.org/Crisis_of_the_Third_Century/) Following the murder of Emperor Alexander Severus in 235 CE, military commanders vied for power, leading to the fragmentation of imperial unity, including the temporary secession of the Gallic Empire in the west (260–274 CE) and the Palmyrene Empire in the east (260–273 CE).[](https://www.bbc.co.uk/history/ancient/romans/thirdcenturycrisis_article_01.shtml) This political anarchy stemmed from the failure of the Severan dynasty's reliance on praetorian and provincial armies, which prioritized loyalty to generals over the state, fostering a cycle of short-lived reigns averaging about two years.[](https://oyc.yale.edu/history/hist-210/lecture-2)\n\nEconomic disintegration exacerbated the crisis, driven primarily by rampant currency debasement and resulting hyperinflation. Emperors, facing revenue shortfalls from endless wars and bribes to secure military allegiance, progressively reduced the silver content in the *denarius* from nearly pure under Augustus to less than 5% by the mid-third century, alloying it with base metals like copper to mint more coins.[](https://www.thecollector.com/inflation-third-century-crisis/) This monetary manipulation flooded the economy with devalued currency, causing prices in Roman Egypt—a region with detailed papyrus records—to surge by factors of up to 1,000% between 250 and 275 CE for staples like wheat, while trade networks collapsed due to insecurity and hoarding.[](https://money.visualcapitalist.com/currency-and-the-collapse-of-the-roman-empire/) Agricultural output declined as landowners abandoned marginal estates amid heavy taxation to fund the military, which consumed over half the imperial budget, further straining urban populations and prompting rural self-sufficiency.[](https://mises.org/mises-daily/inflation-and-fall-roman-empire)\n\nThe Plague of Cyprian, erupting around 250 CE and persisting until about 270 CE, inflicted demographic catastrophe, killing an estimated 10–20% of the empire's population—potentially 5–10 million people—through symptoms including fever, gastrointestinal hemorrhage, and gangrenous extremities, likely caused by smallpox or hemorrhagic Ebola-like viruses.[](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33399666/) [](https://www.worldhistory.org/article/992/plague-of-cyprian-250-270-ce/) The epidemic, possibly introduced via trade routes or military campaigns, decimated legionary ranks, with units reduced by up to two-thirds in affected provinces, compelling greater recruitment of barbarian foederati whose integration diluted Roman discipline and loyalty.[](https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-roman-archaeology/article/plague-of-cyprian-a-revised-view-of-the-origin-and-spread-of-a-3rdc-ce-pandemic/4DD870D473D3DDB1DF0328CE2C7E3280) Economic repercussions included labor shortages that accelerated urban depopulation and disrupted tax collection, as bishops like Cyprian of Carthage documented mass burials and societal breakdown, attributing the disaster to divine disfavor but highlighting the empire's vulnerability to unchecked pandemics without modern sanitation or medical knowledge.[](https://romanempiretimes.com/the-plague-of-cyprian-disease-faith-and-crisis-in-the-roman-empire/)\n\nMilitary and administrative failures compounded these woes, as the empire's overextended bureaucracy—swollen to manage vast territories—suffered from corruption, with provincial governors exploiting civilians through extortion and soldiers engaging in banditry, eroding public trust and local governance.[](https://www.history.com/articles/8-reasons-why-rome-fell) The army, ballooning to around 500,000 men by the late third century, prioritized internal suppression over border defense, yet proved ineffective against simultaneous threats due to poor logistics and reliance on ill-trained levies.[](https://www.bbc.co.uk/history/ancient/romans/thirdcenturycrisis_article_01.shtml) Reforms under Diocletian (r. 284–305 CE), including the tetrarchy and price edicts, temporarily stabilized the east but imposed rigid controls that stifled innovation and deepened divisions, setting the stage for persistent western fragility into the fifth century.[](https://www.historyskills.com/classroom/ancient-history/crisis-of-the-third-century/)\n\nBy the fourth and fifth centuries, these internal dynamics persisted, with inflation recurring despite Aurelian's partial monetary restoration in 274 CE, and administrative centralization under Constantine (r. 306–337 CE) failing to halt the west's slide toward fiscal insolvency.[](https://www.unrv.com/economy/currency-debasement.php) The cumulative effect—political fragmentation, economic contraction, and demographic decline—undermined the empire's resilience, making it susceptible to external pressures without addressing root causes like unsustainable militarism and elite detachment from provincial realities.[](https://dacollege.org/uploads/stdmat/history-sem2-cc4-Roman-Empire%2520_2.pdf)\n\n### Barbarian Migrations and New Political Orders\n\n![Europe and the Near East at 476 AD.png][float-right]\n\nThe barbarian migrations, often termed the Völkerwanderung or Migration Period, commenced around 375 AD when the Huns, advancing from the Eurasian steppes, displaced Gothic tribes across the Danube River into Roman territories.[](https://worldhistoryedu.com/what-were-the-barbarian-invasions/) This influx exposed Roman military vulnerabilities, culminating in the Gothic victory at the Battle of Adrianople in 378 AD, where Emperor Valens perished alongside two-thirds of the Eastern field army.[](https://www.historyskills.com/classroom/year-8/yr-8-barbarian-migrations-reading/) Subsequent waves included the 406 AD crossing of the frozen Rhine by Vandals, Alans, and Suebi, who ravaged Gaul before penetrating Hispania, exploiting Roman internal divisions and economic strains from overreliance on slave labor and debased currency.[](https://worldhistoryedu.com/what-were-the-barbarian-invasions/)\n\nIn 410 AD, Visigothic forces under Alaric I sacked Rome, the first such breach in eight centuries, signaling the erosion of central imperial authority in the West.[](https://study.com/academy/lesson/migration-period-overview-history-barbarian-invasions.html) The Vandals, under Gaiseric, seized Carthage in 439 AD after traversing Spain and Sicily, establishing a maritime kingdom in North Africa that controlled vital grain supplies and raided Italy, sacking Rome again in 455 AD.[](https://www.livescience.com/who-were-the-vandals) These movements were not mere destructive hordes but organized tribal confederations seeking arable land and Roman subsidies, accelerated by climatic shifts like the Late Antique Little Ice Age and population pressures beyond the limes.[](https://worldhistoryedu.com/what-were-the-barbarian-invasions/)\n\nThe migrations fostered new political orders as Germanic elites superimposed rule over Romano-provincial populations, forming successor kingdoms that blended customary law with Roman administrative remnants. The Visigoths, initially foederati in Aquitaine from 418 AD, shifted to Hispania after Frankish pressure, unifying the peninsula under kings like Euric (466–484 AD) with a capital at Toledo by 507 AD, issuing codes like the Breviary of Alaric that preserved Roman legal traditions for subjects.[](https://study.com/academy/lesson/the-visigoths-in-spain-rome.html)\n\n![Theoderic_Quarter_Siliqua_80000847.jpg][center]\n\nOstrogothic Italy, under Theoderic the Great (r. 493–526 AD), exemplified continuity: commissioned by Eastern Emperor Zeno, Theoderic ousted Odoacer and governed as patricius, retaining the Roman Senate, prefectures, and tax systems while settling 100,000–200,000 Goths on choice lands, fostering a dual society of Arian rulers and Catholic subjects with minimal ethnic strife during his reign.[](https://courses.lumenlearning.com/atd-herkimer-westerncivilization/chapter/theoderic-the-great/) In contrast, the Franks under Clovis I (r. 481–511 AD) achieved unification by defeating Syagrius's Roman remnant at Soissons in 486 AD and Alemanni at Tolbiac circa 496 AD, followed by his conversion to Nicene Christianity, baptizing 3,000 warriors and aligning with Gallo-Roman clergy against Arian rivals, laying foundations for the Merovingian dynasty.[](https://www.historyhit.com/how-clovis-created-a-basis-for-french-national-identity-in-the-5th-and-6th-centuries/)\n\nThese kingdoms endured through pragmatic accommodations: Germanic warrior elites, numbering perhaps 5–10% of populations, monopolized military roles while Romans handled civics, though recurrent civil wars and external threats like Hunnic incursions at Chalons in 451 AD tested stability.[](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8445445/) By 476 AD, when Odoacer deposed the puppet emperor Romulus Augustulus, the Western imperial framework had yielded to federated polities, marking a transition from universal empire to regional monarchies rooted in conquest yet sustained by inherited Roman infrastructure.[](https://www.usu.edu/markdamen/1320hist\u0026civ/chapters/08romfal.htm)\n\n### Byzantine Empire as Eastern Successor\n\n![Europe and the Near East at 476 AD](./_assets_/Europe_and_the_Near_East_at_476_AD.png)\nThe deposition of Romulus Augustulus by Odoacer in 476 marked the effective end of centralized Roman rule in the West, yet the Eastern Roman Empire, governed from Constantinople, maintained unbroken continuity as the Roman state.[](https://shadowsofconstantinople.com/roman-continuity/) This persistence stemmed from the East's more robust administrative framework, denser urbanization, and greater wealth relative to the West, which buffered it against the full brunt of Germanic migrations and internal upheavals.[](https://acoup.blog/2022/01/28/collections-rome-decline-and-fall-part-ii-institutions/) Emperors such as Zeno (r. 474–491) and Anastasius I (r. 491–518) consolidated power by managing fiscal reforms and containing threats like the Ostrogoths and Persians, preserving Roman imperial institutions including the bureaucracy, taxation systems, and professional armies.[](https://historyguild.org/byzantium-the-new-rome/)\n\nUnder Justinian I (r. 527–565), the empire actively asserted its role as the Roman successor through ambitious reconquests aimed at restoring lost western territories. General Belisarius led campaigns that vanquished the Vandal Kingdom in North Africa by 534 and initiated the reconquest of Ostrogothic Italy in 535, temporarily reclaiming Rome in 536 and expanding Byzantine control across the Mediterranean.[](https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/the-byzantine-state-under-justinian-i-justinian-the-great) These efforts, though straining resources and provoking plagues and rebellions, underscored the empire's self-conception as the legitimate heir to Augustus, evidenced by Justinian's codification of Roman law in the *Corpus Juris Civilis* (529–534), which systematized imperial edicts and jurisprudence for enduring administrative use.[](https://www.thecollector.com/justinian-i-recapture-rome/)\n\nThe Byzantine state's longevity in this transitional era derived from adaptive strategies, including a defensible capital fortified by walls and seas, a theme-based military organization emerging post-reconquests, and diplomatic maneuvering that often forestalled invasions.[](http://byzantium.filminspector.com/2019/12/how-did-byzantine-empire-last-so-long.html) While the West fragmented into barbarian kingdoms, the East's retention of Hellenistic-Roman cultural synthesis, Orthodox Christianity as a unifying force, and economic hubs like Constantinople—handling trade volumes exceeding those of fragmented Europe—ensured institutional resilience amid late antique crises.[](https://www.diplomacy.edu/histories/byzantine-diplomacy-the-elixir-of-longevity/) By 600, despite losses to Slavs and Avars, the empire had weathered the period's transformations, positioning itself as the primary custodian of Roman legacy into the medieval era.[](https://www.khanacademy.org/humanities/world-history/medieval-times/byzantine-empire/a/the-rise-of-the-byzantine-empire)\n\n## Early Middle Ages (c. 600–1000)\n\n### Economic Disruption and Localism\n\n![Early medieval village](./_assets_/Fr%C3%BChmittelalterliches_Dorf.jpg)\nThe early Middle Ages witnessed profound economic disruption stemming from the lingering effects of the Western Roman Empire's collapse in 476 CE, compounded by recurrent crises such as the Plague of Justinian (541–750 CE), which caused tens of millions of deaths across the Mediterranean and Europe, severely depopulating regions and undermining agricultural and commercial capacities.[](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6926030/) This pandemic, recurring in waves, exacerbated labor shortages and fiscal strains, contributing to the paralysis of urban economies and the abandonment of marginal lands.[](https://origins.osu.edu/connecting-history/covid-justinianic-plague-lessons) In Italy, Lombard invasions beginning in 568 CE further fragmented trade routes and administrative structures, leading to a breakdown in the distribution of goods like African ceramics and olive oil that had sustained Roman-era commerce.[](https://shadowsofconstantinople.com/the-lombard-destruction-of-roman-italy/)\n\nUrban centers, once hubs of Roman trade and administration, experienced sharp declines; for instance, Rome's population dwindled from approximately 500,000 in the 5th century to 20,000–50,000 by the late 6th century, reflecting reduced commercial activity and migration to rural areas.[](https://www.quora.com/How-and-why-did-Rome-experience-such-massive-depopulation-after-the-Western-Empires-collapse-from-1-million-people-to-only-50-000-in-the-Middle-Ages) Long-distance trade networks, reliant on secure Mediterranean shipping and overland routes, contracted markedly, with archaeological evidence showing a scarcity of imported amphorae and coins in Western Europe from the 6th to 8th centuries.[](https://jmboehm.github.io/coins.pdf) Overall European population estimates hovered around 25–30 million circa 600 CE, down from Roman peaks, limiting surplus production and market exchange.\n\nIn response, economic organization shifted toward localism, characterized by self-sufficient agrarian estates or manors that prioritized subsistence over market-oriented production. Rural households in the Carolingian period (c. 750–900 CE) were predominantly self-reliant, producing food, tools, and textiles internally, with limited reliance on external markets as evidenced by estate inventories like the Polyptych of Irminon (c. 812 CE). Lords managed demesnes worked by dependent peasants, fostering localized exchange at seasonal fairs rather than extensive trade, which supported political fragmentation by tying economic security to personal protection networks.[](https://www.jstor.org/stable/202330) This manorial structure, rooted in late Roman villas but intensified by insecurity, enabled adaptation to depopulation and instability but stifled broader economic integration until the 10th century.[](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/355859623_Early_European_towns_The_development_of_the_economy_in_the_Frankish_realm_between_dynamism_and_deceleration_AD_500-1100)\n\n### Islamic Conquests and Eurasian Interactions\n\n![Map of the expansion of the Caliphate](./_assets_/Map_of_expansion_of_Caliphate.svg.png)\n\nThe Islamic conquests commenced following the death of Muhammad in 632 CE, under the Rashidun Caliphs, who directed military campaigns against neighboring empires weakened by prolonged conflicts.[](https://www.britannica.com/topic/Rashidun) Abu Bakr, the first caliph (632–634 CE), suppressed internal rebellions known as the Ridda Wars before initiating external expansions, dispatching forces into southern Iraq by 633 CE.[](https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-1/pages/11-2-the-arab-islamic-conquests-and-the-first-islamic-states) Under Umar (634–644 CE), Arab armies defeated Byzantine forces at the Battle of Yarmouk in 636 CE, securing Syria and Palestine by 638 CE, and captured Egypt by 642 CE after the fall of Alexandria.[](https://www.the-map-as-history.com/origins-of-islam-and-the-arabo-muslim-empire/the-arab-conquests-634-751) Concurrently, Persian territories succumbed, with the Sassanid capital Ctesiphon taken in 637 CE and the last Sassanid emperor Yazdegerd III killed in 651 CE, completing the conquest of the Sassanid Empire by 654 CE.[](https://www.worldhistory.org/image/14212/map-of-the-islamic-conquests-in-the-7th-9th-centur/)\n\nThe Umayyad Caliphate (661–750 CE) extended these gains, conquering North Africa from 647 CE onward, reaching the Atlantic by 709 CE after overcoming Berber resistance.[](https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-1/pages/11-2-the-arab-islamic-conquests-and-the-first-islamic-states) In 711 CE, Tariq ibn Ziyad led a Berber-Arab force across the Strait of Gibraltar, defeating Visigothic King Roderic at the Battle of Guadalete, which facilitated the rapid subjugation of most of the Iberian Peninsula by 718 CE, establishing Al-Andalus.[](https://historymedieval.com/the-umayyad-caliphate-invasion-of-spain/) Umayyad forces then crossed the Pyrenees into Francia, raiding Aquitaine and advancing northward until halted by Frankish Mayor of the Palace Charles Martel at the Battle of Tours (Poitiers) on October 10, 732 CE, where an estimated 1,500–20,000 Muslims were killed, marking a decisive check on further European incursions.[](https://www.britannica.com/event/Battle-of-Tours-732)\n\nThese conquests profoundly affected Eurasian interactions, severing direct overland trade routes between Western Europe and the East, contributing to economic fragmentation in the Mediterranean.[](https://human.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Humanities/Humanities%253A_Prehistory_to_the_15th_Century_%28Lumen%29/08%253A_Western_European_Civilization_During_the_Middle_Ages/8.03%253A_The_Rise_of_Islam_Carolingian_Europe_New_Kingdoms_and_a_Revived_Byzantium) The Byzantine Empire, already strained, lost its Levantine provinces, Egypt, and North Africa, reducing its revenue base by two-thirds and forcing a defensive posture along Anatolia, with failed Arab sieges of Constantinople in 674–678 CE and 717–718 CE.[](https://www.khanacademy.org/humanities/world-history/medieval-times/spread-of-islam/a/the-rise-of-islamic-empires-and-states) In Europe, the Iberian foothold enabled ongoing raids into Frankish territories, such as the 735 CE incursion into Burgundy, while Byzantine-Arab frontier wars persisted, fostering militarized themes in Anatolia and influencing Carolingian alliances against common threats.[](https://www.the-map-as-history.com/origins-of-islam-and-the-arabo-muslim-empire/the-arab-conquests-634-751) The conquests also integrated steppe elements through alliances with Berbers and later Turkic groups in Central Asia, though direct Eurasian steppe interactions remained limited until Abbasid times.[](https://en.unesco.org/silkroad/knowledge-bank/arab-conquest)\n\n### Carolingian Unification and Its Fragmentation\n\nPepin III, known as Pepin the Short, deposed the last Merovingian king Childeric III in 751 and was anointed king of the Franks by Pope Stephen II in 754, marking the rise of the Carolingian dynasty and initial unification of Frankish territories under effective Carolingian rule.[](https://fiveable.me/the-middle-ages/unit-4) This act consolidated power previously held by Carolingian mayors of the palace, ending the Merovingian shadow kingship and centralizing authority through papal endorsement and military control over Austrasia, Neustria, and Burgundy.[](https://courses.lumenlearning.com/atd-herkimer-westerncivilization/chapter/the-rise-of-charlemagne/)\n\nPepin's son, Charlemagne (r. 768–814), inherited the throne jointly with his brother Carloman in 768 but became sole ruler after Carloman's death in 771, launching extensive conquests that expanded the Frankish realm into a vast empire. Key campaigns included the subjugation of the Lombards in Italy by 774, securing papal territories; the conquest of Saxony through wars from 772 to 804, involving forced conversions and mass executions such as at Verden in 782; incorporation of Bavaria in 788; and raids into Muslim Spain, establishing the Spanish March.[](https://warfarehistorynetwork.com/article/charlemagne-warlord-of-the-franks/) By 800, when Pope Leo III crowned him emperor in Rome on Christmas Day, Charlemagne's domain encompassed modern-day France, Germany, the Low Countries, Switzerland, Austria, northern Italy, and parts of Spain and Croatia, uniting diverse Germanic, Romance, and Slavic peoples under a single Christian rulership. \n\n![Frankish Empire expansion](._assets_/Frankish_Empire_481_to_814-en.svg.png)\n\nTo govern this expansive and heterogeneous empire, Charlemagne implemented administrative reforms, including the capitularies—royal ordinances standardizing laws, coinage, and weights—and the missi dominici, pairs of royal envoys (one lay, one clerical) dispatched annually from 802 to inspect local counts, enforce justice, collect taxes, and suppress corruption, thereby extending central oversight into peripheral regions.[](https://www.8thcentury.com/the-kings-hands/) These measures, alongside promotion of monastic scriptoria and the Carolingian minuscule script, fostered administrative uniformity and a modest revival of learning, though reliant on existing Roman and Germanic customs rather than wholesale innovation.[](https://fiveable.me/the-middle-ages/unit-4) Military obligations were systematized via the host and ban, requiring freemen to serve based on land holdings, sustaining the empire's defenses but straining resources over time.\n\nCharlemagne's son Louis the Pious (r. 814–840) inherited the undivided empire but faced rebellions from nobles and his own sons, leading to repeated partitions via the Ordinatio Imperii in 817, which designated Lothair as co-emperor while granting subkingdoms to Pepin, Louis the German, and Charles.[](https://www.thoughtco.com/louis-i-profile-1789099) Louis's death in 840 triggered a three-year civil war among his sons, culminating in the Treaty of Verdun on August 10, 843, which fragmented the realm: Lothair I received Middle Francia (including Italy, Burgundy, and the Low Countries); Louis the German took Eastern Francia (precursor to Germany); and Charles the Bald acquired Western Francia (precursor to France).[](https://resources.saylor.org/wwwresources/archived/site/wp-content/uploads/2012/08/HIST201-1.1.3-FallofCarolingianEmpire-FINAL.pdf) This division, driven by fraternal rivalries and the logistical challenges of ruling vast territories without robust infrastructure, undermined unified governance, as local counts and bishops increasingly asserted autonomy amid weakened central authority.\n\n![Carolingian divisions 843](._assets_/Carolingian_territorial_divisions%252C_843.png)\n\nSubsequent treaties accelerated fragmentation: the Treaty of Prüm in 855 split Lothair's lands among his heirs, while the Treaty of Meerssen in 870 repartitioned Middle Francia after Lothair II's death, further dispersing territories.[](https://www.dbu.edu/mitchell/medieval-resources/carolingian_overview.html) By the late 9th century, external pressures like Viking raids and internal revolts eroded Carolingian legitimacy, with non-dynastic rulers emerging in East Francia by 911 and West Francia by 987, effectively ending the empire's cohesive phase and paving the way for feudal decentralization.[](https://courses.lumenlearning.com/atd-herkimer-westerncivilization/chapter/the-end-of-the-carolingians/) The Carolingian model's emphasis on personal loyalty and itinerant kingship proved unsustainable against geographic scale and succession disputes, contributing to the political multiplicity of early medieval Europe.\n\n### External Invasions: Vikings, Magyars, and Saracens\n\n![Europe in 900 AD](./_assets_/Europe_in_900_AD.png)\n\nDuring the ninth and tenth centuries, the fragmented polities of post-Carolingian Europe endured relentless assaults from seafaring Vikings originating from Scandinavia, mounted Magyar warriors from the eastern steppes, and Saracen forces raiding from Mediterranean bases, which collectively strained royal authority and accelerated the devolution of power to local lords.[](https://pressbooks.cuny.edu/thebirthofeuropefall2022/chapter/chapter-1-the-high-middle-ages/) These incursions, peaking between approximately 793 and 955, exploited weak borders and internal divisions, resulting in widespread depopulation, economic disruption, and fortified settlements, while prompting defensive innovations like burhs in England and comital militias in Francia.[](https://heritage-history.com/site/hclass/catholic_knowledge/saints_heroes/kingdoms/vikings.pdf)\n\nViking raids commenced with the assault on Lindisfarne monastery on June 8, 793, where Norse warriors plundered the Northumbrian island, slaughtering monks and seizing treasures, an event chronicled as omens preceding calamity in Anglo-Saxon annals.[](https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/visit/places/lindisfarne-priory/History/viking-raid/) This marked the onset of systematic predation by Danish, Norwegian, and Swedish fleets targeting coastal monasteries and trade centers across Britain, Ireland, Francia, and beyond, with over 200 recorded attacks by 900.[](https://www.historyextra.com/period/viking/guide-viking-raid-lindisfarne-what-happened-when/) Escalation occurred in 865 when the Great Heathen Army, a coalition of perhaps 3,000-5,000 warriors, landed in East Anglia, overwintering and methodically conquering Northumbria by 867, executing King Ælla, then East Anglia in 869, and partitioning Mercia by 874, establishing the Danelaw zone of Norse settlement and law.[](https://www.historic-uk.com/HistoryUK/HistoryofEngland/Great-Heathen-Army/) In Francia, Vikings besieged Paris in 845 and again in 885-886, extorting 700 pounds of silver, before the 911 Treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte ceded Rollo territory around Rouen, founding the Duchy of Normandy as a buffer against further incursions.[](https://thevikingherald.com/article/the-treaty-of-saint-clair-sur-epte-how-the-vikings-became-normans/1093) These operations, driven by overpopulation, trade opportunities, and shipbuilding prowess enabling rapid strikes, inflicted annual tributes like Danegeld and fostered Viking principalities in Dublin and York, though Christianization and assimilation curbed raiding by the early eleventh century.[](https://www.lifeinnorway.net/lindisfarne-viking-raid/)\n\nMagyar tribes, Finno-Ugric nomads allied with Pechenegs, migrated westward around 895, occupying the Carpathian Basin after displacing Bulgars and Avars, from which they launched mounted archery raids penetrating deep into Germany, Italy, and Burgundy, amassing plunder through hit-and-run tactics superior to fragmented Frankish heavy cavalry.[](https://about-history.com/hungarian-conquests-of-europe/) Annual campaigns from 900 onward sacked cities like Bremen in 915 and Basel in 917, with forces estimated at 10,000-20,000, exploiting Carolingian civil wars to demand tribute until their decisive defeat at the Battle of Lechfeld on August 10, 955, where Otto I's coalition of 8,000 heavy infantry and knights annihilated Lél and Bulcsú's army near Augsburg, crucifying leaders and halting western expeditions.[](https://warfarehistorynetwork.com/article/the-battle-of-lechfeld-955-ad/) This loss, compounded by internal strife and Bulgarian pressures, compelled Árpád dynasty leaders to consolidate in Pannonia, adopting sedentary agriculture and Christianity by 1000 under Stephen I, transforming raiders into the Kingdom of Hungary.[](https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/history/magyars-invade-italy-saxony-and-bavaria)\n\nSaracen raids, conducted by Aghlabid and later Fatimid fleets from North Africa and Sicily, targeted southern Italy and the western Mediterranean coasts, beginning with the 827 expedition to Sicily that, despite initial setbacks, culminated in the Byzantine stronghold of Taormina's fall on November 902, establishing Muslim emirates ruling the island until Norman reconquest.[](https://www.italyonthisday.com/2019/08/the-arab-conquest-of-sicily-902.html) From Sicilian bases, corsairs ravaged Campania and Calabria, sacking Rome's environs in 846 and prompting Leo IV's walls, while a splinter group founded Fraxinetum (modern La Garde-Freinet) in Provence around 940, from which 200-300 warriors raided Alpine passes, capturing pilgrims and merchants until their expulsion by William of Arles' coalition in 972-975.[](https://blog.nationalmuseum.ch/en/2024/10/the-saracen-raids-of-early-medieval-switzerland/) These operations, motivated by jihad, slave trade, and naval dominance with up to 100 ships per fleet, disrupted pilgrimage routes and commerce, extorting protection from Genoa and Pisa, but waned as Byzantine and Norman counteroffensives reclaimed territories by the late tenth century.[](https://www.academia.edu/30235215/Fragile_Borders_beyond_the_Strait_Saracen_Raids_on_the_Italian_Peninsula_8th_11th_century_A_D_)\n\nCollectively, these invasions rendered centralized governance untenable, as kings like Charles the Bald subdivided realms via the 843 Treaty of Verdun, yielding to vassalage systems where counts and dukes raised levies independently, a causal shift from imperial unity to feudal particularism evidenced by rising castle counts from 20 in 800 to over 3,000 by 1000.[](https://courses.emediava.org/onlineCourses/WHI/mod8top2_content_invasions/story_content/external_files/mod8top2_content_invasions.pdf) Empirical records, including annals like the Annals of St. Bertin detailing Viking sieges and Magyar tributes, underscore how scarcity of coinage and literacy reflected survival priorities over administration.[](https://admisiones.unicah.edu/uploaded-files/XT7g0g/2OK046/a_history_of_the_vikings.pdf)\n\n### Role of the Church and Monastic Centers\n\n![Pope Gregory I](./_assets_/Gregory_I_-_Antiphonary_of_Hartker_of_Sankt_Gallen.jpg)\n\nThe Church served as a primary institution of continuity and authority during the political fragmentation following the Roman Empire's collapse, maintaining administrative structures, legal traditions, and moral guidance across Europe from approximately 600 to 1000.[](https://www.worldhistory.org/Medieval_Church/) Bishops and clergy often filled administrative roles in local governance, leveraging Roman-era infrastructure like dioceses to coordinate charity, justice, and community welfare amid barbarian kingdoms' instability.[](https://www.worldhistory.org/Medieval_Church/) Papal influence expanded under figures like Pope Gregory I (r. 590–604), who reformed ecclesiastical administration, dispatched missionaries such as Augustine of Canterbury in 597 to convert Anglo-Saxon England, and asserted Rome's spiritual primacy over bishops in Gaul and elsewhere.[](https://scs.libguides.com/medievalchristian/popes/gregory) Gregory's efforts, including his *Pastoral Care* (c. 590), emphasized clerical duties and pastoral oversight, strengthening the Church's role in countering heresy and paganism.[](https://www.1517.org/articles/church-fathers-gregory-the-great-pastoral-pope)\n\nMonastic centers, guided by rules like St. Benedict's (c. 530), emerged as vital hubs for spiritual discipline, economic self-sufficiency, and intellectual preservation, with communities such as those in Ireland and Francia copying classical and patristic texts in scriptoria to safeguard knowledge from antiquity.[](https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/monasticism-in-western-medieval-europe) By the 8th century, Irish and Anglo-Saxon monks, including figures like Columbanus (d. 615), established monasteries across Europe, promoting literacy and evangelism; these institutions housed libraries that preserved works by Cicero, Virgil, and early Church Fathers, preventing total loss during invasions.[](https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/monasticism-in-western-medieval-europe) Monks' labor-intensive routines, including manual work (*ora et labora*), supported agricultural advancements, such as introducing three-field crop rotation and heavy plows in regions like the Rhine Valley, boosting yields and enabling surplus production that sustained local economies.[](https://www.returntoorder.org/2018/07/the-influence-of-monasteries-on-the-middle-ages-economic-boom/)\n\nIn the Carolingian era (c. 751–888), the Church intertwined with royal power under Charlemagne (r. 768–814), who convened synods like the Council of Frankfurt (794) to standardize doctrine and liturgy, fostering a revival of learning known as the Carolingian Renaissance.[](https://courses.lumenlearning.com/atd-herkimer-westerncivilization/chapter/charlemagnes-reforms/) Scholars like Alcuin of York (d. 804), invited to Charlemagne's court, reformed monastic education, establishing palace schools and scriptoria at centers like Aachen and Tours that produced uniform Carolingian minuscule script, facilitating wider dissemination of texts.[](https://courses.lumenlearning.com/atd-herkimer-westerncivilization/chapter/charlemagnes-reforms/) Monasteries under Carolingian oversight, such as Fulda (founded 744), expanded economic activities through land reclamation, viticulture, and trade networks, contributing to regional stability and the integration of Christian ethics into governance.[](https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/cambridge-history-of-medieval-monasticism-in-the-latin-west/monastic-economics-in-the-carolingian-age/26439C3BE3FDB96B92167857FC941956) Missionaries like St. Boniface (d. 754) further extended Church influence, organizing dioceses in Germania and aligning tribal conversions with Frankish expansion.[](https://theyorkhistorian.com/2015/06/23/the-role-of-monasteries-in-the-carolingian-empire/) This symbiosis of ecclesiastical and secular authority helped mitigate the era's disruptions, preserving Roman administrative legacies while adapting to feudal realities.[](https://www.worldhistory.org/Medieval_Church/)\n\n### Incremental Technological and Agricultural Advances\n\nDuring the Early Middle Ages, agricultural practices in Europe gradually shifted from the Roman-era two-field system, where one field lay fallow while the other was cultivated, to the more efficient three-field rotation by the 8th century, with one field sown in winter crops like wheat or rye, another in spring crops such as oats or barley, and the third left fallow.[](https://www.britannica.com/topic/three-field-system) [](https://engines.egr.uh.edu/episode/26) This innovation, evidenced in Carolingian capitularies and manorial records from the 9th century, increased arable land use from 50% to 67% and boosted soil fertility through legume rotations, yielding modest productivity gains of 10-20% in northern regions.[](https://www.britannica.com/topic/three-field-system) Concurrently, the heavy wheeled plow (carruca), featuring a moldboard to turn heavy clay soils, emerged in Slavic and Germanic areas between the 7th and 10th centuries, enabling cultivation of previously marginal northern European lands unsuitable for lighter Roman ard plows.[](https://journals.librarypublishing.arizona.edu/uahistjrnl/article/id/586/download/pdf/) Archaeological finds, such as plowshares from 8th-century sites in Denmark and Poland, confirm its incremental adoption, which reduced labor needs per acre by allowing deeper tillage but required communal coordination for drainage and ox teams.[](https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/history/heavy-plow-helps-increase-agricultural-yields)\n\nHarnessing improvements complemented these changes; the rigid horse collar, first depicted in a circa 800 AD illuminated manuscript from Trier, distributed traction force across the horse's shoulders rather than throat, permitting faster plowing speeds (up to twice that of oxen) and reducing animal exhaustion.[](https://www.academia.edu/116311848/The_origin_of_the_horse_collar_2022) Though originating in Chinese designs centuries earlier, its European adaptation around the 9th century—evidenced in Frankish inventories—facilitated draft horse use on heavier soils, with horses comprising up to 20% of traction animals by 1000 AD in monastic estates.[](https://www.academia.edu/116311848/The_origin_of_the_horse_collar_2022) These agricultural tweaks, often disseminated via monastic scriptoria and Carolingian reforms under Charlemagne (r. 768–814), sustained yields amid climatic challenges like the Late Antique Little Ice Age's extension into the 6th-7th centuries, preventing total collapse but limiting surpluses to elite domains.[](https://human.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/History/World_History/Western_Civilization_-_A_Concise_History_II_%28Brooks%29/01%253A_The_Crusades_and_the_High_Middle_Ages/1.06%253A_The_Medieval_Agricultural_Revolution)\n\nTechnologically, the proliferation of watermills marked a key continuity from Late Antiquity, with over 6,000 documented in England by 1086 but roots in 8th-century Frankish and Anglo-Saxon expansions for grain milling and early fulling.[](https://www.britannica.com/technology/history-of-technology/From-the-Middle-Ages-to-1750) Monastic centers, such as those at Jarrow (founded 681) and Fulda (744), engineered vertical-wheel mills powered by diverted streams, grinding up to 1,000 kg of grain daily per site and freeing labor from manual querns, though efficiency varied with seasonal flows and maintenance costs.[](https://www.britannica.com/technology/history-of-technology/From-the-Middle-Ages-to-1750) Ironworking advanced incrementally with bloomery furnaces refined by the 9th century, producing higher-quality tools like sickles and scythes, as seen in Viking Age Scandinavian hoards, while tidal and overshot mills appeared experimentally in coastal monasteries by 800 AD.[](https://www.britannica.com/technology/history-of-technology/From-the-Middle-Ages-to-1750) These developments, pragmatic responses to labor shortages post-plague and invasions, emphasized hydraulic and animal power over speculative inventions, laying groundwork for High Medieval intensification without implying a \"revolution\" in the period.[](https://www.britannica.com/technology/history-of-technology/From-the-Middle-Ages-to-1750)\n\n## High Middle Ages (c. 1000–1300)\n\n### Population Growth and Commercial Revival\n\n![Venice, a key center of medieval trade](./_assets_/Venice_city_scenes_-_in_St._Mark's_square_-_St_Mark's_Basilica_\\(11002237996\\))\n\nFollowing the relative stability after the cessation of major invasions around 1000 AD, Europe's population expanded significantly, rising from roughly 40 million to at least 80 million by the early 14th century.[](https://www.economics.utoronto.ca/munro5/L02MedievalPopulationC.pdf) This growth, estimated to have averaged about 0.1-0.2% annually, outpaced earlier medieval rates and supported economic expansion.[](https://www.economics.utoronto.ca/munro5/L02MedievalPopulationC.pdf) Key drivers included climatic improvements during the Medieval Warm Period (c. 950–1250), which extended growing seasons and enabled cultivation of marginal lands in northern Europe, alongside reduced famine frequency due to fewer volcanic disruptions and stable solar activity.[](https://depositsmag.com/2017/07/18/a-warming-medieval-climate-supports-a-revolution-in-agriculture/)\n\nAgricultural productivity surged through innovations like the widespread adoption of the three-field crop rotation system by the 11th century, which divided fields into thirds—one for autumn grains like wheat or rye, one for spring crops like oats or legumes, and one left fallow—effectively increasing arable land use by 50% compared to the prior two-field method and enhancing soil nitrogen via legumes.[](https://human.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/History/World_History/Western_Civilization_-_A_Concise_History_II_%28Brooks%29/01%253A_The_Crusades_and_the_High_Middle_Ages/1.06%253A_The_Medieval_Agricultural_Revolution) Complementary technologies, such as the heavy wheeled plow with moldboard for turning heavy clay soils and the horse collar harness (replacing inefficient ox yokes), allowed faster plowing and greater draft power, further boosting yields on northern Europe's challenging terrains.[](https://depositsmag.com/2017/07/18/a-warming-medieval-climate-supports-a-revolution-in-agriculture/) These advancements generated food surpluses, lowering mortality rates—particularly infant and child deaths—and enabling larger families, as evidenced by parish records showing fertility rates sustaining the demographic upswing.[](https://courses.lumenlearning.com/atd-herkimer-westerncivilization/chapter/daily-medieval-life/)\n\nSurpluses fueled commercial revival, with rural-to-urban migration swelling towns; by 1300, urban populations comprised 10-15% of Europe's total, up from near negligible levels centuries prior.[](https://www.economics.utoronto.ca/munro5/L02MedievalPopulationC.pdf) Trade networks reemerged, linking northern Europe via the Rhine and Baltic routes to Mediterranean ports, where Italian city-states like Venice and Genoa dominated exchanges with Byzantine and Islamic merchants for spices, silks, and Eastern luxuries.[](https://www.worldhistory.org/article/1301/trade-in-medieval-europe/) Inland hubs, notably the Champagne fairs (c. 1180–1300), facilitated six annual cycles of bulk goods trading—wool, cloth, and metals—drawing Flemish, Italian, and Provencal participants and standardizing payments through early credit instruments.[](https://www.worldhistory.org/article/1301/trade-in-medieval-europe/)\n\nFinancial innovations underpinned this commerce: Italian bankers in the 12th century developed bills of exchange, allowing merchants to transfer funds across distances without coin transport, mitigating risks from fragmented currencies and banditry.[](https://www.worldhistory.org/article/1301/trade-in-medieval-europe/) Guilds regulated quality and monopolies in burgeoning industries like textiles and metalwork, while minting reforms and royal protections for markets encouraged investment.[](https://www.worldhistory.org/article/1301/trade-in-medieval-europe/) By the 13th century, this system supported proto-capitalist growth, with per capita output rising amid specialization, though vulnerabilities to harvest failures persisted, foreshadowing 14th-century crises.[](https://www.economics.utoronto.ca/munro5/L02MedievalPopulationC.pdf)\n\n### Feudalism: Mutual Obligations and Economic Realities\n\n![Three orders of medieval society][float-right]\n\nFeudalism encompassed reciprocal obligations between lords and vassals, formalized through ceremonies of homage and fealty, where vassals pledged loyalty and military service in exchange for fiefs—grants of land providing economic sustenance.[](https://courses.lumenlearning.com/atd-herkimer-westerncivilization/chapter/feudalism/) A key primary account of these duties appears in a 1020 letter from Bishop Fulbert of Chartres to Duke William V of Aquitaine, outlining vassal responsibilities including honoring the lord, protecting him from enemies, refraining from alliances against him, avoiding harm to his family or forbidding his men access to fiefs, providing counsel, defending the lord's reputation, aiding in his needs without betrayal, returning the fief if stripped, and performing required services faithfully.[](https://islesproject.wordpress.com/2007/11/26/500ce-1400-primary-sources-illustrating-european-feudalism/) Lords, in turn, were bound to maintain the vassal's security, administer justice, and support him in necessities, ensuring the relationship's mutuality rather than mere subjugation.\n\nThese obligations underpinned a military hierarchy essential for defense amid 9th- to 11th-century invasions, with vassals typically owing around 40 days of annual knight service, supplemented by castle garrison duties or expeditionary aid.[](https://paulbuddehistory.com/europe/feudalism-and-vassalage/) Financial aids arose for lordly ransoms, knighting eldest sons, or marrying daughters, often calibrated to the fief's value, as seen in 12th-century French customs where such payments could equal a year's revenue.[](https://historyguild.org/medieval-life/) The heritability of fiefs, advanced by the 877 Capitulary of Quierzy under Charles the Bald, allowed beneficiaries to inherit conditional land grants upon royal approval, stabilizing vassalage across generations but fragmenting central authority as local powers entrenched.[](https://hal.science/hal-01471188v1)\n\nEconomically, feudalism intertwined with manorialism, organizing agrarian production on self-sufficient estates where lords held demesne lands cultivated by unfree peasants (serfs or villeins) obligated to labor services, typically two to three days weekly, alongside customary rents in kind or money.[](https://courses.lumenlearning.com/atd-herkimer-westerncivilization/chapter/the-manor-system/) Manors, prevalent from the Carolingian era onward, divided into lordly demesne, peasant holdings, and common pastures or woods, with the three-field rotation system—adopted widely by the 9th century—enhancing yields by fallowing one-third of arable land annually, boosting output over two-field methods.[](https://www.historyworld.net/history/Manorialsystem/575) The 1086 Domesday Book survey of England recorded over 13,000 manors, valuing the realm at approximately £72,000 annually, reflecting inputs like plough-teams (averaging 2-3 per manor in fertile regions) and outputs dominated by grain, with serf households numbering 100,000-200,000 contributing to subsistence amid limited trade.[](https://www.domesdaybook.net/domesday-book/data-terminology/money-values/values) [](https://www.medievalists.net/2010/08/the-domesday-economy-of-england-1086/)\n\nIn the High Middle Ages, population expansion from circa 1000—reaching 30-40 million in Western Europe by 1300—strained manorial resources, prompting commutations of labor into cash rents and nascent commercialization, yet core economic realities persisted in localized, agrarian dependencies vulnerable to harvests and seigneurial exactions.[](https://fiveable.me/the-middle-ages/unit-5) Regional variations abounded: England's post-1066 impositions yielded detailed records absent in France, while Italian city-states diverged toward proto-capitalism, underscoring feudalism's adaptability rather than uniformity.[](https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-economic-history/article/determinants-of-manorial-income-in-domesday-england-evidence-from-essex/5D8A7F3F33D8653E8B88FD91F9232403)\n\n### Rise of Monarchies and Common Law Traditions\n\n![Bayeux Tapestry scene depicting Norman leadership](./_assets_/Bayeux_Tapestry_scene44_William_Odo_Robert.jpg)\n\nDuring the High Middle Ages, European monarchies transitioned from fragmented feudal structures toward greater centralization, particularly in England and France, where kings leveraged administrative innovations and military successes to expand authority and diminish noble autonomy. In France, the Capetian dynasty, beginning with Hugh Capet in 987, initially controlled a modest domain around Île-de-France, but rulers like Louis VI (r. 1108–1137) initiated expansion by subduing vassals and securing oaths of loyalty, laying groundwork for territorial growth.[](http://www.flowofhistory.com/category/export/html/212.html) Philip II Augustus (r. 1180–1223) accelerated this by confiscating Norman territories from King John of England in 1204, effectively doubling the royal domain and incorporating regions like Normandy and Anjou, which funded further administrative centralization.[](https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.1515/9780691213057-008/html) By 1300, the Capetian holdings had expanded to approximate much of modern France's core, enabling professional bureaucracies and taxation systems that reduced reliance on feudal levies.[](https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.1515/9780691213057-008/html)\n\nIn England, the Norman Conquest of 1066 established a monarchy with unprecedented documentary control, exemplified by the Domesday Book of 1086, but High Medieval consolidation peaked under Henry II (r. 1154–1189), who reformed justice to assert royal supremacy over local customs. Henry dispatched itinerant justices to apply uniform procedures nationwide, introducing assizes—royal writs for land disputes resolved via jury trials like the Grand Assize of 1179, where 12 knights determined possession based on sworn testimony rather than feudal combat.[](https://www.bbc.co.uk/history/british/middle_ages/henryii_law_01.shtml) These circuits standardized legal practices in royal courts, fostering the common law tradition: a body of judge-made precedents derived from customary law, applicable across the realm and overriding manorial or ecclesiastical jurisdictions.[](https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/henry-ii/henry-ii-and-the-creation-of-the-english-common-law/FADA3B5AD4E01E89B8718119F620B1A2) Criminal justice evolved similarly, with presentment juries of local freemen indicting suspects, shifting from ordeal-based trials toward evidentiary processes that prioritized royal oversight.[](https://users.ssc.wisc.edu/~rkeyser/?page_id=625)\n\nThe common law's endurance stemmed from its adaptability and royal enforcement, as centralized courts like the King's Bench, formalized around 1178, recorded decisions in yearbooks that established binding precedents.[](https://www.law.berkeley.edu/wp-content/uploads/2017/11/CommonLawCivilLawTraditions.pdf) This system curtailed baronial power by offering tenants direct recourse to the king against seigneurial abuses, as seen in possessory assizes protecting against wrongful dispossession.[](https://www.bbc.co.uk/history/british/middle_ages/henryii_law_01.shtml) King John's fiscal exactions provoked the Magna Carta in 1215, where barons compelled concessions affirming that no freeman could be punished except by lawful judgment of peers or country—clauses 39 and 40 embedding due process into monarchy, limiting arbitrary rule while preserving royal courts as arbiters.[](https://www.parliament.uk/about/living-heritage/evolutionofparliament/originsofparliament/birthofparliament/overview/magnacarta/magnacartasignificant/) Reissued under Henry III, it reinforced common law's role in constraining monarchs to legal norms, influencing later constitutional developments without undermining central authority.[](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/380126406_The_Impact_of_the_Magna_Carta_on_Medieval_English_Politics)\n\nThese parallel developments—territorial monarchies in France and procedural common law in England—reflected pragmatic responses to feudal inefficiencies, enabling rulers to extract resources and administer justice uniformly, though full absolutism awaited later eras. In contrast, the Holy Roman Empire's elective system perpetuated fragmentation, underscoring how inherited customs and geography shaped divergent paths to sovereignty.[](https://facstaff.bloomu.edu/mhickey/state_and_society_in_the_high_mi.htm)\n\n### Crusades: Defensive Imperatives and Strategic Results\n\nThe Crusades arose amid escalating threats to Christian territories in the eastern Mediterranean, following centuries of Islamic military expansion that had subdued vast regions previously under Byzantine or Christian control. From the 7th century onward, Arab forces conquered Syria (634–638), Egypt (639–642), and North Africa (647–709), while Umayyad armies overran Visigothic Spain by 711, establishing al-Andalus and conducting raids into Francia as far as Poitiers in 732.[](https://scholarsarchive.byu.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1805\u0026context=ccr) By the 11th century, the Seljuk Turks, having consolidated power in Persia, invaded Anatolia, culminating in their decisive victory over Byzantine Emperor Romanos IV Diogenes at the Battle of Manzikert on August 26, 1071, which enabled the rapid Turkic conquest of most of Anatolia and imperiled Constantinople itself.[](https://www.britannica.com/event/Battle-of-Manzikert) This defeat prompted Byzantine Emperor Alexios I Komnenos to appeal for Western military aid in 1095, highlighting the existential danger to Eastern Christendom from Seljuk advances that disrupted pilgrimage routes to Jerusalem and desecrated holy sites.[](https://sourcebooks.web.fordham.edu/source/urban2-5vers.asp)\n\nPope Urban II's address at the Council of Clermont on November 27, 1095, framed the response as a defensive imperative, urging knights to relieve Byzantine pressures, protect endangered Christians, and reclaim access to sacred lands amid reports of Muslim atrocities against pilgrims, including the destruction of the Church of the Holy Sepulchre in 1009 under Fatimid Caliph al-Hakim and subsequent Seljuk restrictions.[](https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/imperialism/readings/urban.html) Urban emphasized reconquering territories lost to prior invasions rather than initiating unprovoked aggression, promising spiritual indulgences for participants while invoking the duty to counter jihadist incursions that had already reduced Christian populations in the Levant through conquest, taxation, or forced conversion.[](https://scholarsarchive.byu.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1805\u0026context=ccr) This call resonated amid Europe's own vulnerabilities from Viking, Magyar, and Saracen raids, positioning the Crusades as a unified Christian bulwark against further erosion of frontiers, with initial mobilizations drawing from Francia, Normandy, and the Rhineland.[](https://historymedieval.com/revisiting-the-crusades-defense-faith-and-survival/)\n\nThe First Crusade (1096–1099) achieved unexpected strategic successes, traversing Anatolia to capture Nicaea in June 1097 with Byzantine assistance, then besieging and taking Antioch on June 3, 1098, before storming Jerusalem on July 15, 1099, with an estimated 12,000–15,000 Crusader combatants overcoming Fatimid defenses.[](https://www.medievalists.net/2025/03/crusades-history-1095-1500/) These victories enabled the establishment of four principal Crusader states: the County of Edessa (1098), Principality of Antioch (1098), Kingdom of Jerusalem (1099), and County of Tripoli (1102), which served as forward buffers, securing trade corridors and pilgrimage access while compelling Muslim forces to divert resources eastward.[](https://historyguild.org/the-curious-creation-of-the-crusader-states/)\n\n![Europe mediterranean 1190](./_assets_/Europe_mediterranean_1190.jpg)\n\nStrategically, these states facilitated military innovations, including the formation of orders like the Knights Templar (founded 1119) and Hospitallers, which pioneered heavy cavalry tactics and fortified coastal enclaves such as Crac des Chevaliers, deterring immediate Seljuk reconquest and buying decades for European consolidation.[](https://www.worldhistory.org/article/1273/the-crusades-consequences--effects/) The Crusades stimulated Mediterranean commerce, with Italian city-states like Venice and Genoa gaining trading privileges in Crusader ports, fostering economic revival through spices, silks, and naval logistics that enhanced royal revenues via taxes and tariffs.[](https://study.com/academy/lesson/results-and-impact-of-the-crusades.html) For Byzantium, early gains recaptured western Anatolia temporarily, but escalating tensions culminated in the Fourth Crusade's sack of Constantinople in 1204, fragmenting the empire and hastening its vulnerability to Ottoman successors.\n\nSubsequent Crusades yielded mixed results: the Second (1147–1149) failed to retake Edessa after its fall in 1144, while the Third (1189–1192) under Richard I of England and Philip II of France recaptured coastal Acre in 1191 but could not restore inland Jerusalem, lost to Saladin in 1187.[](https://www.historic-uk.com/HistoryUK/HistoryofEngland/Timeline-Crusades/) Despite ultimate territorial losses—Antioch in 1268, Jerusalem's remnants in 1244, and Acre in 1291—the campaigns fragmented Muslim unity, notably weakening Ayyubid and Mamluk coordination, and preserved European frontiers by redirecting jihadist focus southward.[](https://fiveable.me/the-middle-ages/unit-8/major-crusades-outcomes/study-guide/SE5MUJ58C4KPJyfe) Long-term, they accelerated state-building in Europe through feudal levies and papal taxation, while cultural exchanges introduced Arabic-preserved classical texts, underpinning later scholastic advances without which Christendom's defensive posture might have collapsed sooner.[](https://blaydes.people.stanford.edu/sites/g/files/sbiybj24621/files/media/file/blaydes_crusades.pdf)\n\n![Crac des chevaliers](./_assets_/Crac_des_chevaliers_syria.jpeg)\n\n### Scholasticism, Universities, and Rational Inquiry\n\nScholasticism emerged in the 11th century as a method of philosophical and theological inquiry that integrated Aristotelian logic with Christian doctrine, emphasizing dialectical reasoning to resolve apparent contradictions between faith and reason.[](https://classicalliberalarts.com/history-of-education/scholastic-philosophy-the-classical-method-for-attaining-wisdom-and-truth/) Anselm of Canterbury (1033–1109), often regarded as the father of scholasticism, advanced the principle of *fides quaerens intellectum* (\"faith seeking understanding\"), exemplified in his *Proslogion* (c. 1078), where he employed rational argumentation for proofs of God's existence, such as the ontological argument.[](https://www.thecollector.com/important-philosophers-scholastic-method/) This approach marked a shift from mere scriptural exegesis toward systematic analysis, fostering intellectual rigor amid the High Middle Ages' institutional growth.[](http://www.logicmuseum.com/wiki/Scholasticism)\n\nPeter Abelard (1079–1142) further refined scholastic methodology in his *Sic et Non* (c. 1121), compiling 158 theological questions with contradictory patristic citations to demonstrate the need for rational reconciliation through dialectic, urging students to distinguish between literal and contextual meanings in authorities.[](https://sourcebooks.web.fordham.edu/source/Abelard-SicetNon-Prologue.asp) Abelard's disputational technique—posing a question, presenting arguments pro and con, and synthesizing a resolution—influenced teaching practices and highlighted human reason's role in clarifying divine truths, though it drew condemnation for perceived over-reliance on logic at the Council of Soissons in 1121.[](https://exterminatingangel.com/sic-et-non/) By the 13th century, scholasticism reached maturity with Thomas Aquinas (1225–1274), whose *Summa Theologica* (1265–1274) systematically addressed over 600 theological issues using Aristotelian categories, arguing that faith and reason are harmonious, with philosophy as the \"handmaid\" of theology.[](https://press.princeton.edu/books/paperback/9780691191799/thomas-aquinass-summa-theologiae) Aquinas's work, drawing on newly translated Greek and Arabic texts, synthesized empirical observation and deduction, laying groundwork for later scientific methodologies by insisting on verifiable premises.[](https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aquinas/)\n\nThe rise of universities institutionalized scholastic inquiry, providing structured environments for debate and specialization. The University of Bologna, established around 1088 as a guild of law students, focused on Roman and canon law, granting the first known degree in 1158 and emphasizing practical rational application to governance.[](https://historiana.eu/historical-content/source-collections/bologna-and-the-rise-of-medieval-universities) The University of Paris, formalized c. 1150 under ecclesiastical oversight, became a hub for theology and arts, where masters like Albertus Magnus (c. 1200–1280) taught empirical sciences alongside metaphysics, attracting thousands of students by the 13th century.[](https://www.hps.cam.ac.uk/students/research-guide/medieval-early-modern-universities) Oxford University, emerging c. 1096 and structured by 1167 after scholars fled Paris, paralleled Paris in scholastic theology but emphasized natural philosophy, with figures like Roger Bacon (c. 1214–1292) advocating experimental verification in optics and alchemy.[](https://www.topuniversities.com/blog/10-oldest-universities-world) These institutions adopted the scholastic trivium (grammar, logic, rhetoric) and quadrivium (arithmetic, geometry, music, astronomy), culminating in disputations where students defended theses publicly, promoting adversarial reasoning over rote memorization.[](http://www.logicmuseum.com/wiki/Scholasticism)\n\nRational inquiry flourished through the *quaestio* format, involving *lectio* (textual exposition) and *disputatio* (formal debate), which trained minds in causal analysis and evidence-based conclusions, countering fideism by subjecting doctrines to logical scrutiny.[](https://classicalliberalarts.com/history-of-education/scholastic-philosophy-the-classical-method-for-attaining-wisdom-and-truth/) This method preserved classical texts via monastic scriptoria and Toledo translations (post-1085), enabling advancements like Aquinas's five proofs for God's existence grounded in motion, causation, and contingency—arguments rooted in observable realities rather than pure assertion.[](https://www.gotquestions.org/Scholasticism.html) Despite condemnations, such as the 1277 Parisian prohibitions against radical Aristotelianism, scholasticism's emphasis on universals, particulars, and probabilistic knowledge influenced proto-empiricism, with over 30 universities founded by 1300 across Europe, enrolling tens of thousands and disseminating rational tools that persisted beyond the Middle Ages.[](https://cepr.org/voxeu/columns/history-pre-modern-european-universities-nutshell) Critics from Franciscan voluntarists like Duns Scotus (c. 1266–1308) challenged excessive rationalism, yet the era's output—thousands of quaestiones and commentaries—demonstrated causal realism in linking theological axioms to empirical data, advancing Western intellectual traditions.[](https://www.thecollector.com/important-philosophers-scholastic-method/)\n\n### Gothic Innovations in Art and Architecture\n\n![Canterbury Cathedral stained glass window](./_assets_/Canterbury_Cathedral%252C_window_nXV_detail_\\(46220634195\\))\n\nThe Gothic style emerged in northern France during the early 12th century, marking a departure from the heavier Romanesque architecture through structural advancements that emphasized verticality, light, and intricate decoration. Abbot Suger of Saint-Denis initiated this shift with the reconstruction of the Abbey Church of Saint-Denis between 1137 and 1144, incorporating elements like the ambulatory completed around 1140–1144, which featured innovative vaulting and larger windows to symbolize divine illumination.[](https://smarthistory.org/birth-of-the-gothic-abbot-suger-and-the-ambulatory-at-st-denis/) These changes allowed for taller interiors and more expansive use of stained glass, transforming churches into luminous spaces intended to evoke spiritual transcendence.[](https://www.thecollector.com/gothic-architecture-saint-denis-basilica/)\n\nKey architectural innovations included pointed arches, ribbed vaults, and flying buttresses, which collectively enabled the construction of soaring cathedrals with thinner walls and expansive window areas. Pointed arches, systematized in Gothic designs, distributed weight more efficiently than Romanesque rounded arches, facilitating greater height and stability.[](https://smarthistory.org/gothic-architecture-an-introduction/) Ribbed vaults, evident in early Gothic structures from the 12th century, provided skeletal support that concentrated loads on piers, freeing wall surfaces for clerestory windows filled with stained glass depicting biblical narratives and saints.[](https://fiveable.me/history-of-architecture/unit-4/gothic-cathedrals/study-guide/BiexEtUs4npn5Ybw) Flying buttresses, external arched supports, further reinforced walls against outward thrust, as seen in later High Gothic examples like Chartres Cathedral (begun 1194), allowing for unprecedented scale—such as naves reaching over 100 feet in height—and intricate tracery that filtered light into colored patterns.[](https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/gothic-art)\n\nIn art, Gothic innovations paralleled these structural advances, with sculpture evolving toward greater naturalism and emotional expressiveness compared to Romanesque rigidity. Portal jamb statues on cathedrals, such as those at Reims Cathedral (c. 1240s), depicted elongated figures with flowing drapery and individualized features, reflecting heightened observation of human anatomy and movement.[](https://www.theartstory.org/movement/gothic-art-and-architecture/) Stained glass windows, a hallmark medium, advanced in technical precision during the 12th and 13th centuries, using pot-metal glass and silver stain for vibrant hues and grisaille effects, narrating theological stories accessible to illiterate congregations.[](https://smarthistory.org/gothic-architecture-an-introduction/) Illuminated manuscripts, like those from Gothic scriptoria, incorporated delicate line work and gold leaf to illustrate texts with dynamic compositions, influencing panel painting precursors. These artistic developments, integrated into architecture, underscored a synthesis of engineering prowess and theological symbolism, peaking in the 13th century amid economic prosperity and ecclesiastical patronage.[](https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/gothic-art)\n\n### Military Reforms and Engineering Feats\n\n![Krak des Chevaliers, exemplifying advanced crusader castle engineering](./_assets_/Crac_des_chevaliers_syria.jpeg)\nDuring the High Middle Ages, European warfare increasingly emphasized heavy cavalry, with knights serving as the primary shock troops in feudal armies. This development was facilitated by selective breeding of large warhorses, known as destriers, capable of bearing armored riders, alongside improvements in saddles featuring high cantles and pommels by the 12th century that stabilized lance charges.[](https://www.britannica.com/technology/military-technology/The-age-of-cavalry-c-400-ce-1350) Armor evolved from chain mail hauberks covering to the knees in the 11th century to include padded gambesons and early plate elements like breastplates by around 1250, enhancing rider protection during charges.[](https://www.britannica.com/technology/military-technology/The-age-of-cavalry-c-400-ce-1350) Feudal obligations required vassals to provide equipped knights for campaigns, typically 40 days of service annually, forming the core of armies that prioritized mobility and impact over massed infantry.[](https://deremilitari.org/2014/07/caste-skill-and-training-the-evolution-of-cohesion-in-european-armies-from-the-middle-ages-to-the-sixteenth-century/)\n\nThe Crusades spurred organizational reforms, notably the establishment of military orders such as the Knights Templar in 1119, which provided disciplined, permanently available heavy cavalry units unbound by feudal terms.[](https://www.historic-uk.com/HistoryUK/HistoryofEngland/The-Knights-Templar/) These orders, including the Hospitallers, acted as vanguard forces in battles like Montgisard in 1177, where Templars bolstered outnumbered crusader lines against Saladin's forces, and developed logistical innovations like early banking to fund prolonged expeditions.[](https://www.history.com/articles/knights-templar-facts-crusades-wealth) Tactics adapted to Eastern influences, incorporating combined arms with archers and infantry to support cavalry, though heavy horse remained decisive in open engagements.[](https://www.worldhistory.org/article/1281/the-armies-of-the-crusades/)\n\nEngineering feats centered on fortifications and siege machinery, transforming defensive and offensive capabilities. Following the Norman Conquest of 1066, England saw the rapid construction of up to 500 motte-and-bailey castles in the first two decades, featuring earthen mounds topped with wooden keeps for quick deployment to control territory.[](https://www.essentially-england.com/norman-castles.html) These evolved into stone structures with curtain walls, towers for flanking fire, and moats by the 12th century, designed to withstand battering rams and mining.[](https://www.britannica.com/technology/military-technology/The-castle) In the Levant, crusader castles like Krak des Chevaliers, fortified by the Hospitallers from 1142 and expanded into concentric designs in the 13th century, integrated double walls and glacis slopes to repel assaults, housing garrisons of up to 2,000.[](https://www.worldhistory.org/Krak_Des_Chevaliers/)\n\nOffensively, the counterweight trebuchet, introduced to Europe in the 12th century likely via Byzantine or Islamic intermediaries, revolutionized sieges by hurling stones over 200 meters with greater accuracy and power than traction models, as evidenced in the 1191 Siege of Acre.[](https://uccshes.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2012/09/the-invention-of-the-counterweight-trebuchet.pdf)[](https://www.britannica.com/technology/trebuchet) These machines, requiring teams of engineers and up to 100 operators, countered stone fortifications but prompted further defensive innovations like machicolations for dropping projectiles.[](https://www.britannica.com/technology/military-technology/The-castle) Such advancements reflected a causal dynamic where offensive engineering drove defensive responses, sustaining an arms race that defined medieval military architecture.[](https://www.historynet.com/how-to-capture-a-castle-with-siegecraft/)\n\n## Late Middle Ages (c. 1300–1500)\n\n### Demographic Catastrophes: Famine, Plague, and Warfare\n\nThe Late Middle Ages (c. 1300–1500) were marked by a confluence of environmental, epidemiological, and military pressures that triggered profound population declines across Europe, reducing the continent's inhabitants from approximately 79 million in 1300 to around 50–60 million by 1400.[](https://medium.com/migration-issues/notes-on-medieval-population-geography-fd062449364f)[](https://www.economics.utoronto.ca/munro5/L02MedievalPopulationC.pdf) These catastrophes stemmed from climatic shifts initiating the Little Ice Age, which disrupted agricultural yields, combined with the unchecked spread of *Yersinia pestis* via trade networks and the direct and indirect toll of interstate conflicts that hindered recovery. Empirical records, including manorial rolls and tax assessments, document sharp contractions in arable land under cultivation and labor availability, underscoring the causal chain from resource strain to mass mortality.[](https://cepr.org/voxeu/columns/famine-war-and-climate-change-middle-ages-russia-ukraine-war)\n\nThe Great Famine of 1315–1317 initiated this era of crisis, triggered by prolonged heavy rains and cooler temperatures that caused widespread crop failures, particularly of wheat and oats, across northern Europe from Ireland to Poland. Livestock murrains further eroded food supplies by decimating herds, leading to inflated grain prices—up to eight times pre-famine levels in England—and reports of cannibalism, infanticide, and urban unrest in chronicles from the period. Mortality estimates range from 5–12% in affected regions due to starvation compounded by opportunistic infections like pneumonia and dysentery, with England's population dropping from about 4.5–5 million to roughly 4 million as weakened survivors succumbed to subsequent illnesses.[](https://www.science.smith.edu/climatelit/the-great-famine/)[](https://britishfoodhistory.com/2020/09/09/the-great-famine-1315-1317/) This event exposed vulnerabilities in the high medieval population boom, where marginal lands had been overexploited, but it also prompted short-term adaptations like diversified cropping, though these proved insufficient against the ensuing plague.\n\nThe Black Death, peaking between 1347 and 1351, amplified the demographic collapse, claiming 30–60% of Europe's population through bubonic, pneumonic, and septicemic forms of plague transmitted by fleas on black rats along Silk Road and Mediterranean trade routes. Contemporary accounts, such as those by Florentine chronicler Agnolo di Tura, describe entire villages depopulated, with mortality rates exceeding 50% in urban centers like London and Paris; overall, 25–50 million deaths occurred, halving England's populace to 2–2.5 million by the 1370s.[](https://www.americanscientist.org/article/the-bright-side-of-the-black-death)[](https://hosted.lib.uiowa.edu/histmed/plague/)[](https://quod.lib.umich.edu/f/frag/9772151.0006.001/--black-death-bodies) Recurrences every 10–20 years until the early 15th century prevented rebound, as malnourished survivors faced eroded immunity and abandoned fields, though genetic studies of plague victims confirm the bacterium's role without evidence of exaggerated contemporary fears.[](https://www.khanacademy.org/humanities/world-history/medieval-times/disease-and-demography/a/disease-and-demograpy)\n\nProtracted warfare, notably the Hundred Years' War (1337–1453) between England and France, inflicted additional losses through combat, scorched-earth tactics, and induced famines, with indirect effects like disrupted harvests and refugee spreads of disease compounding plague-era declines. French demography suffered acutely, with regional estimates indicating up to 50% population loss by mid-century from chevauchées—raiding expeditions that devastated countryside economies—though direct battle deaths numbered in the tens of thousands, far outweighed by civilian attrition from siege warfare and mercenary predation.[](https://www.tutorchase.com/answers/ib/history/how-did-the-hundred-years--war-affect-french-demographics)[](https://aithor.com/essay-examples/consequences-of-the-hundred-years-war-between-england-and-france-research-paper) Similar patterns emerged in conflicts like the Wars of the Roses (1455–1487) in England, where noble feuds fragmented authority and stalled agricultural recovery. Collectively, these factors stalled Europe's population at depressed levels into the 15th century, fostering labor shortages that reshaped social structures but rooted in verifiable ecological and pathogenic pressures rather than institutional failure alone.[](https://www.economics.utoronto.ca/munro5/L02MedievalPopulationC.pdf)\n\n### Shifts in Economy and Social Mobility\n\nThe Black Death, peaking between 1347 and 1351, decimated Europe's population by an estimated 30 to 60 percent, fundamentally disrupting the agrarian economy dominated by manorial production and servile labor.[](https://eh.net/encyclopedia/the-economic-impact-of-the-black-death/) [](https://www.aeaweb.org/articles?id=10.1257/jel.20201639) Labor scarcity immediately elevated the value of surviving workers, causing real wages to rise sharply—doubling in England by the late fourteenth century as demand outstripped supply in agriculture and crafts.[](https://eh.net/encyclopedia/the-economic-impact-of-the-black-death/) [](https://history.wustl.edu/news/how-black-death-made-life-better) Landlords, facing idle estates and resistance to traditional obligations, increasingly commuted fixed labor dues into monetary rents, shifting from coerced services to contractual arrangements that incentivized productivity.[](https://eh.net/encyclopedia/the-economic-impact-of-the-black-death/) This transition weakened the manorial system's rigidity, as excess land relative to labor encouraged specialization in higher-yield activities like sheep farming for wool exports, particularly in England and the Low Countries.[](https://eh.net/encyclopedia/the-economic-impact-of-the-black-death/)\n\nSerfdom, the legal bondage tying peasants to lords' lands with hereditary obligations, eroded across Western Europe as demographic collapse empowered laborers to negotiate or evade customary dues.[](https://www.aeaweb.org/articles?id=10.1257/jel.20201639) By the mid-fifteenth century, servile tenure had largely vanished in England, replaced by copyhold tenancies offering greater security and heritability, while in France and Germany, peasants often bought out remaining feudal burdens amid ongoing revolts like the English Peasants' Revolt of 1381.[](https://eh.net/encyclopedia/the-economic-impact-of-the-black-death/) [](https://www2.gwu.edu/~iiep/assets/docs/papers/2020WP/JedwabIIEP2020-14.pdf) These changes fostered upward social mobility for some, enabling former serfs to accumulate capital through wage work, urban migration, or small-scale leasing, though noble privileges and guild restrictions limited widespread ascent and preserved hierarchical inequalities.[](https://history.wustl.edu/news/how-black-death-made-life-better) [](https://webhelper.brown.edu/decameron/plague/effects/social.php)\n\nCommercial networks, already expanding in the High Middle Ages, adapted to these pressures through innovations in finance and trade, with Italian merchant-bankers like the Medici pioneering bills of exchange to mitigate risks in cross-regional commerce by the 1400s.[](https://ehistory.osu.edu/articles/medieval-banking-twelfth-and-thirteenth-centuries) Wool and cloth exports from northern Europe surged, fueling proto-capitalist ventures and urban growth despite intermittent disruptions from the Hundred Years' War (1337–1453) and recurring plagues.[](https://academic.oup.com/book/39318/chapter/350575657) Overall, the era's economic realignment—marked by wage labor's prevalence, monetary flows over barter, and market responsiveness—laid groundwork for early modern capitalism, as abundance of resources relative to people rewarded efficiency over coercion.[](https://eh.net/encyclopedia/the-economic-impact-of-the-black-death/) [](https://www.aeaweb.org/articles?id=10.1257/jel.20201639)\n\n### Dynastic Wars and Territorial Consolidation\n\nThe Hundred Years' War (1337–1453), rooted in Edward III of England's dynastic claim to the French throne through his mother Isabella, daughter of Philip IV of France, intertwined feudal land disputes in Aquitaine with succession crises following the Capetian dynasty's extinction in the male line in 1328.[](https://www.history.ox.ac.uk/did-hundred-years-war-against-france-strengthen-sense-english-national-identity) [](https://www.historyskills.com/classroom/year-8/hundred-years-war-reading/) Intermittent campaigns, including English victories at Crécy (1346) and Poitiers (1356) via longbow tactics, gave way to French resurgence under Charles VII, aided by Joan of Arc's campaigns from 1429, culminating in the decisive artillery triumph at Castillon (1453). This expelled English forces from all continental holdings except Calais, enabling the Valois kings to dismantle appanages held by refractory nobles, impose permanent taxes like the *taille*, and foster a standing army of 15,000–20,000 by Louis XI's reign (1461–1483), thereby centralizing territorial authority and curtailing feudal fragmentation.[](https://www.cliffsnotes.com/study-notes/22605776) \n\nThe war's exhaustion in England precipitated the Wars of the Roses (1455–1487), a protracted dynastic feud between the Lancastrian and Yorkist branches of the Plantagenet house, exacerbated by Henry VI's mental incapacity and noble over-might enabled by bastard feudalism's indentured retinues. Major clashes, such as Towton (1461) with up to 28,000 fatalities and Bosworth Field (1485) where Richard III fell to Henry Tudor's 5,000-strong force, eliminated rival claimants and depleted magnate power bases; Henry VII's Tudor accession unified Yorkist and Lancastrian claims via his mother's lineage, while attainders and marriage alliances like that to Elizabeth of York in 1486 consolidated the crown's fiscal and judicial dominance, reducing private armies and affirming parliamentary consent for taxation.[](https://www.worldhistory.org/article/1498/causes-of-the-wars-of-the-roses/) [](https://www.tutorchase.com/answers/a-level/history/how-did-the-wars-of-the-roses-shape-the-development-of-the-english) This internal pacification redirected resources toward Tudor absolutism, marking a shift from baronial confederacies to royal sovereignty over England's insular territory.\n\nParallel processes unfolded elsewhere: in Iberia, the 1469 marriage of Ferdinand II of Aragon to Isabella I of Castile forged a dynastic union of crowns, enabling joint forces to besiege Granada, the last Nasrid emirate, which surrendered on January 2, 1492 after a ten-year campaign involving 50,000–80,000 troops and naval blockades, thus integrating the peninsula's taifas into a Christian realm while expelling or converting Muslim and Jewish populations to enforce religious homogeneity.[](https://www.thecollector.com/marriage-of-ferdinand-and-isabella/) [](https://courses.lumenlearning.com/atd-herkimer-westerncivilization/chapter/the-reconquista/) In the Holy Roman Empire's patchwork, Habsburg rulers like Frederick III (r. 1452–1493) and Maximilian I (r. 1493–1519) prioritized marital diplomacy—such as Maximilian's 1477 union with Mary of Burgundy, inheriting her Low Countries domains worth 4 million florins annually—over outright conquest, incrementally aggregating territories like Tyrol (1363) and Austria's stem duchies to buffer against Ottoman incursions and princely autonomies, laying foundations for composite monarchy amid the Empire's elective decentralization. [](https://fiveable.me/key-terms/ap-euro/habsburg-rule) These conflicts, driven by inheritance laws favoring primogeniture yet prone to female-line disputes, empirically eroded lateral feudal ties, substituting dynastic survival with bureaucratic and military instruments of territorial statehood.[](https://openmedia.yale.edu/projects/iphone/departments/hist/hist202/transcript02.html)\n\n### Papal Schisms and Calls for Reform\n\nThe Avignon Papacy, spanning 1309 to 1377, relocated the papal court to Avignon under French influence following Pope Clement V's flight from Italian unrest, fostering perceptions of corruption through excessive taxation, simony, and clerical immorality amid growing administrative centralization.[](https://www.thefaithfulhistorian.com/p/avignon-papacys-ecclesiastical-reforms) [](https://worldhistoryedu.com/what-was-the-avignon-papacy/) Critics, including figures like William of Ockham, decried the popes' temporal ambitions and dependence on the French monarchy, which alienated northern European rulers and intensified demands for ecclesiastical purification.[](https://pages.uoregon.edu/dluebke/Reformations441/Conciliarism.htm) Efforts at reform during this period, such as curbing pluralism and absenteeism, yielded mixed results but failed to restore trust, as the court's opulence—evidenced by annual revenues exceeding 300,000 florins—contrasted sharply with clerical poverty vows.[](https://www.thefaithfulhistorian.com/p/avignon-papacys-ecclesiastical-reforms)\n\nThe Western Schism erupted in 1378 upon the return to Rome under Pope Gregory XI, whose death prompted the cardinals' election of Urban VI; his abrasive reforms and execution threats alienated the electors, who then installed Clement VII in Avignon, splitting Christendom between Roman and antipapal obediences.[](https://www.academia.edu/7086344/The_Great_Western_Schism_Himanshi_S) [](https://www.diplomacy.edu/histories/renaissance-diplomacy-compromise-as-a-solution-to-conflict/) By 1409, the situation escalated to three concurrent claimants after the Council of Pisa deposed both rivals and elected Alexander V, only for the original popes to persist, dividing loyalties along national lines—France backing Avignon, England and the Empire supporting Rome.[](https://www.academia.edu/7086344/The_Great_Western_Schism_Himanshi_S) This 39-year crisis eroded papal prestige, as secular monarchs like Charles VI of France withdrew obedience and confiscated church properties, reducing papal income by up to 50% in some regions and exposing the institution's vulnerability to political exploitation.[](https://www.academia.edu/7086344/The_Great_Western_Schism_Himanshi_S)\n\nResolution came via the Council of Constance (1414–1418), convened by Emperor Sigismund and initially under Pope John XXIII, which asserted conciliar superiority over the papacy in its *Haec Sancta* decree of 1415, deposing John XXIII, accepting Gregory XII's resignation, and executing the Avignon claimant Benedict XIII's deposition after his flight.[](https://www.papalencyclicals.net/councils/ecum16.htm) [](https://www.gotquestions.org/conciliar-conciliarism.html) The council elected Martin V in 1417, restoring singular occupancy, but its reform agenda—addressing annates, indulgences, and conciliarism—largely faltered, as Martin V dissolved the assembly without binding changes, prioritizing papal autonomy.[](https://www.papalencyclicals.net/councils/ecum16.htm) Conciliarism, theorizing general councils as infallible and capable of deposing errant popes, gained traction among canonists like Pierre d'Ailly but waned post-Constance, undermined by the decree's later contested status.[](https://www.gotquestions.org/conciliar-conciliarism.html)\n\nParallel reform calls emanated from theologians like John Wycliffe (c. 1328–1384), who in works such as *De Potestate Papae* (1379) argued scripture superseded papal decrees, condemned clerical endowments as corrupting—claiming they amassed wealth rivaling kings'—and urged disendowment to enforce apostolic poverty.[](https://www.historytoday.com/archive/john-wycliffe-condemned-heretic) [](https://www.logos.com/grow/john-wycliffe-morning-star/) His English Bible translation (completed c. 1384) and followers, the Lollards, propagated these views through itinerant preaching, criticizing transubstantiation, pilgrimages, and auricular confession as unbiblical, though suppressed by statutes like the 1401 De Haeretico Comburendo, which authorized burnings for heresy.[](https://www.historytoday.com/archive/john-wycliffe-condemned-heretic) In Bohemia, Jan Hus (c. 1369–1415), influenced by Wycliffe, preached against indulgences and simony from 1402, advocating communion in both kinds and lay chalice rights, leading to his condemnation and execution at Constance in 1415 despite safe-conduct assurances.[](https://museeprotestant.org/en/notice/jan-hus-1369-1415-and-the-hussite-wars-1419-1436/) [](https://www.worldhistory.org/Bohemian_Reformation/)\n\nThese movements highlighted systemic issues—clergy holding 30–50% of land in regions like England while neglecting pastoral duties—fueling anticlericalism and proto-national churches, yet the schism's chaos arguably amplified reformist voices by demonstrating papal fallibility without immediate schismatic success.[](https://www.logos.com/grow/john-wycliffe-morning-star/) [](https://museeprotestant.org/en/notice/jan-hus-1369-1415-and-the-hussite-wars-1419-1436/) Empirical records, including conciliar decrees and trial transcripts, reveal genuine grievances rooted in fiscal overreach rather than mere doctrinal dispute, though reform stalled amid power struggles, presaging 16th-century upheavals.[](https://www.papalencyclicals.net/councils/ecum16.htm)\n\n### Intellectual Ferment and Proto-Renaissance\n\nIn the late 14th century, Italian scholars initiated a revival of classical learning that challenged medieval scholastic emphases on theology, prioritizing human potential and secular texts. Francesco Petrarca (1304–1374), often credited as the originator of this humanistic approach, critiqued contemporary scholarship for neglecting ancient Roman and Greek authors, advocating instead for their direct study to foster moral and rhetorical excellence. His *Africa* (c. 1338–1342), an epic poem on Scipio Africanus, and *Canzoniere* (c. 1350s), a collection of vernacular sonnets, exemplified this shift by blending classical imitation with personal introspection, influencing subsequent poets and laying groundwork for Renaissance individualism.[](https://www.britannica.com/biography/Petrarch)[](https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/petrarch)\n\nPhilosophical debates intensified with William of Ockham's (c. 1287–1347) advocacy of nominalism, which posited that universals exist only as mental concepts rather than independent realities, thereby prioritizing empirical observation over metaphysical speculation. This \"Ockham's Razor\"—the principle of parsimony favoring simpler explanations—undermined realist ontologies dominant in earlier scholasticism, promoting skepticism toward unsubstantiated abstractions and foreshadowing empirical methodologies in later science.[](https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ockham/)[](https://philosophy.institute/ancient-medieval/william-of-ockham-nominalism-empiricism/) Ockham's ideas, disseminated through his works like the *Summa Logicae* (c. 1323), contributed to intellectual fragmentation but also encouraged rigorous logical analysis amid the era's crises.\n\nThe Black Death (1347–1351), which killed approximately 30–60% of Europe's population, catalyzed vernacular literature's ascendancy, as authors like Dante Alighieri (1265–1321) and Giovanni Boccaccio (1313–1375) employed local tongues to broaden access to profound ideas, bypassing Latin's clerical monopoly. Dante's *Divine Comedy* (completed 1321), structured as a vernacular allegorical journey through hell, purgatory, and paradise, integrated theology with personal ethics and politics, establishing Italian as a literary medium capable of epic scope and influencing Chaucer's English works.[](https://www.bbc.com/culture/article/20180604-dante-and-the-divine-comedy-he-took-us-on-a-tour-of-hell)[](https://fiveable.me/the-middle-ages/unit-14/dante-emergence-vernacular-literature/study-guide/j4y56nT90tl9LKL4) Boccaccio's *Decameron* (1348–1353), framing 100 tales of human folly and resilience amid plague isolation, highlighted individual agency against fate, reflecting post-epidemic disillusionment with divine order and fostering proto-secular narratives.[](https://www.britannica.com/biography/Giovanni-Boccaccio/The-Decameron)[](https://theconversation.com/guide-to-the-classics-boccaccios-decameron-a-masterpiece-of-plague-and-resilience-143437) These works, born from demographic catastrophe, spurred questioning of ecclesiastical authority and amplified humanistic focus on earthly experience.[](https://gen.medium.com/how-the-black-death-radically-changed-the-course-of-history-644386f5b803)\n\nUniversities proliferated and diversified during the 14th and 15th centuries, with foundations like Charles University in Prague (1348), University of Vienna (1365), and University of Heidelberg (1386) extending beyond traditional theology to law, medicine, and arts, training administrators for emerging bureaucracies. This expansion, partly responsive to papal schisms and state needs, integrated nominalist critiques into curricula, enhancing critical inquiry while generating knowledge networks that bridged medieval and Renaissance paradigms.[](https://cepr.org/voxeu/columns/how-universities-helped-transform-medieval-world)[](https://fiveable.me/the-middle-ages/unit-10/rise-universities-scholasticism/study-guide/xHzFbvoSQKtOYHwC) By 1500, over 60 such institutions operated across Europe, sustaining intellectual momentum despite recurrent plagues.[](https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00309230.2025.2538198?src=)\n\n### Persistent Technological Momentum\n\nDespite the Black Death's devastation, which killed an estimated 25-60% of Europe's population between 1347 and 1351, creating acute labor shortages, technological innovation accelerated as survivors and elites invested in labor-saving devices to boost productivity.[](https://cepr.org/voxeu/columns/black-death-and-industrialisation-lessons-todays-south) [](https://www.medievalists.net/2021/08/the-black-death-and-the-great-transition/) Economic pressures from depopulation shifted resources toward mechanization, with real wages for laborers rising by 50-100% in England and Italy by the late 14th century, enabling capital accumulation for tools and machinery.[](https://cepr.org/voxeu/columns/black-death-and-industrialisation-lessons-todays-south) This momentum built on High Medieval foundations like watermills and heavy plows, but intensified in the Late period, countering notions of stagnation by demonstrating causal links between demographic shocks and inventive responses.[](https://www.medievalists.net/2023/06/medieval-inventions-world/)\n\nIn timekeeping and precision instruments, mechanical clocks emerged as a pivotal advance, with the first verified public escapement clock installed in Milan in 1336, followed by installations in Strasbourg (1354) and London (1370).[](https://www.britannica.com/technology/history-of-technology/From-the-Middle-Ages-to-1750) These verge-and-foliot mechanisms, powered by weights, divided the day into equal hours, facilitating urban coordination for markets, monasteries, and early factories; by 1400, over 3,000 European cities had clocks, enhancing efficiency despite ongoing wars and famines.[](https://www.medievalists.net/2023/06/medieval-inventions-world/) Eyeglasses, invented in Italy around 1286 using convex lenses ground from beryl or glass, proliferated post-plague among scholars and artisans, extending productive lifespans and supporting textual scholarship; production scaled via guild workshops, with exports reaching England by 1326.[](https://interestingengineering.com/lists/18-inventions-of-the-middle-ages-that-changed-the-world)\n\nMetallurgical and industrial techniques advanced markedly, as labor scarcity favored high-output processes. The blast furnace, refined in the Swedish region of Lapphyttan by the 1340s using water-powered bellows to reach 1,200-1,500°C, enabled cast iron production for cannons and tools, supplanting bloomery forges and yielding 10-20 times more output per worker.[](https://www.britannica.com/technology/history-of-technology/From-the-Middle-Ages-to-1750) Vertical windmills, adapted for sawing and grinding, multiplied across the Low Countries and England after 1300, grinding grain at rates up to 10 times manual labor and powering fulling mills for textiles; by 1500, thousands operated, contributing to wool export booms.[](https://brewminate.com/an-analysis-of-medieval-civil-and-military-technological-inventions-and-innovations/)\n\nMaritime and military technologies underscored adaptive momentum. Full-rigged ships with combined square and lateen sails, exemplified by Portuguese caravels from the 1430s, reduced crew needs by 30-50% while enabling Atlantic voyages, driven by fishing and trade demands amid inland depopulation.[](https://epicenter.wcfia.harvard.edu/blog/does-fourteenth-century-black-death-hold-lessons-todays-pandemic) Gunpowder weaponry evolved rapidly, with ribauldequin multi-barrel guns appearing in 1364 and bronze cannons cast via lost-wax methods by 1420, decisively altering sieges like Constantinople's fall in 1453 despite numerical disadvantages.[](https://www.medievalists.net/2023/06/medieval-inventions-world/)\n\nThe printing press with movable type, developed by Johannes Gutenberg in Mainz around 1440 using alloy type and oil-based ink, epitomized this era's culminative thrust, producing 200-300 pages daily versus a scribe's 20-40.[](https://interestingengineering.com/lists/18-inventions-of-the-middle-ages-that-changed-the-world) By 1500, over 1,000 presses operated in Europe, disseminating 20 million volumes and amplifying scholastic texts, nautical charts, and mechanical treatises, thus sustaining intellectual continuity through vernacular translations and cheaper access.[](https://www.thoughtco.com/middle-ages-timeline-1992478) These developments, empirically tied to post-plague incentives rather than exogenous genius, refuted retrospective dismissals of medieval inertia by evidencing cumulative, crisis-fueled progress.[](https://www.medievalists.net/2021/08/the-black-death-and-the-great-transition/)\n\n## Legacy and Historiography\n\n### Enduring Contributions to Western Institutions\n\nThe medieval period established several institutional frameworks that underpin modern Western governance, education, and social welfare, including the university system, principles of limited government and rule of law, representative assemblies, and organized charity through ecclesiastical networks. These developments arose from decentralized feudal structures and ecclesiastical initiatives, which emphasized corporate autonomy, contractual obligations, and communal responsibility, contrasting with the centralized Roman imperial model that had collapsed in the West by 476 AD. Empirical evidence from charters, legal texts, and architectural remnants demonstrates continuity, as these institutions adapted to post-Roman fragmentation while preserving and innovating upon classical and Christian traditions.[](https://dcc.newberry.org/?p=14383)[](https://www.britannica.com/topic/government/The-Middle-Ages)\n\nUniversities originated as self-governing guilds (*universitas*) of masters and students, with the University of Bologna emerging around 1088 as the first in continuous operation, initially focused on reviving Roman civil law through glosses on Justinian's *Corpus Juris Civilis*.[](https://historiana.eu/historical-content/source-collections/bologna-and-the-rise-of-medieval-universities) This model spread northward, as the University of Paris (c. 1150) emphasized theology and arts, and Oxford (c. 1167) followed suit, seceding scholars forming new centers like Cambridge from Oxford and Padua from Bologna.[](https://www.hps.cam.ac.uk/students/research-guide/medieval-early-modern-universities)[](https://academic.oup.com/jeea/article/22/4/1541/7438871) By 1300, over a dozen such institutions dotted Europe, standardizing curricula in the seven liberal arts—trivium (grammar, rhetoric, logic) and quadrivium (arithmetic, geometry, music, astronomy)—which structured Western higher education for centuries, prioritizing disputation and empirical inquiry over rote memorization.[](https://fiveable.me/the-middle-ages/unit-10) Monastic and cathedral schools provided precursors, but universities' corporate charters granted legal autonomy, enabling intellectual independence that fueled scholasticism and later scientific revolutions.[](https://academic.oup.com/jeea/article/22/4/1541/7438871)\n\nLegal institutions evolved through canon law's systematization under Gratian's *Decretum* (c. 1140) and the integration of Roman law, creating adversarial procedures and appellate systems still evident in civil and common law traditions.[](https://www.law.berkeley.edu/research/the-robbins-collection/exhibitions/medieval-law-school/) In England, the Magna Carta of 1215 compelled King John to affirm that no free man could be imprisoned or expropriated except by lawful judgment of peers or the law of the land (clauses 39–40), establishing due process and habeas corpus precursors that restrained monarchical absolutism.[](https://www.parliament.uk/about/living-heritage/evolutionofparliament/originsofparliament/birthofparliament/overview/magnacarta/magnacartasignificant/)[](https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/magna-carta-muse-and-mentor/interpreting-the-rule-of-law.html) These principles influenced parliamentary development, as baronial councils evolved into the English Parliament by 1295, requiring consent for taxation and embodying representative consent over feudal summons.[](https://historyandpolicy.org/policy-papers/papers/magna-carta-and-contemporary-constitutional-change/) Feudalism's vassalage contracts, meanwhile, transitioned to centralized bureaucracies; by the 14th century, monarchs like France's Philip IV built standing armies (e.g., 15,000–20,000 professional infantry by 1328) and fiscal systems, supplanting knightly levies and laying groundwork for nation-states with defined territories and sovereignty.[](https://fiveable.me/the-middle-ages/unit-16/decline-feudalism-rise-centralized-states/study-guide/QeCfQj7CRu4oHlj3)[](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S187704281001147X)\n\nThe Church pioneered enduring welfare institutions, reviving hospitals (*xenodocheia*) from late antique Christian models; Benedictine monasteries from the 6th century onward integrated infirmaries for the sick and poor, with orders like the Knights Hospitaller (founded 1099) expanding during the Crusades to manage facilities treating thousands annually.[](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2359880/)[](https://www.nursing.upenn.edu/nhhc/nurses-institutions-caring/history-of-hospitals/) By 1200, urban hospices in cities like Paris and London provided systematic care, funded by tithes and bequests, predating secular state welfare and influencing modern hospitals' charitable ethos.[](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0953620512002890) Ecclesiastical courts and tithe systems also fostered accountability, as papal legates enforced uniform practices across fragmented polities, contributing to supranational legal norms that paralleled emerging state cohesion.[](https://www.wesleyan.edu/christianitystudies/pathways/development.html) These contributions, rooted in Christian imperatives for almsgiving (e.g., Matthew 25:35–40), sustained literacy and record-keeping via scriptoria, preserving texts that enabled institutional memory amid invasions and plagues.[](https://www.britannica.com/topic/government/The-Middle-Ages)\n\n### Debunking Persistent Myths of Backwardness\n\n![Aachen Cathedral, symbolizing Carolingian architectural innovation](./_assets_/Aachener_Dom_Pfalzkapelle_vom_M%C3%BCnsterplatz_2014.jpg)\n\nThe characterization of the Middle Ages as an era of unrelenting backwardness, epitomized by the \"Dark Ages\" label, emerged in the 14th century with Italian scholar Petrarch, who contrasted the perceived cultural decline after the fall of Rome with the classical past, a view amplified by Enlightenment thinkers seeking to justify their own advancements.[](https://www.historyhit.com/why-were-the-early-middle-ages-called-the-dark-ages/) Modern historiography, drawing on archaeological and documentary evidence, rejects this as an oversimplification, highlighting institutional continuity, technological adaptations, and incremental progress that laid foundations for later European development.[](https://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/the-myth-of-the-dark-ages-ignores-how-classical-traditions-flourished-around-the-world-180982190/) While the early medieval period (c. 500–1000) experienced disruptions from invasions and economic contraction following the Roman collapse, evidence of innovation in agriculture, engineering, and learning counters claims of total stagnation.[](https://historyforatheists.com/2024/09/the-great-myths-15-what-about-the-dark-ages/)\n\nAgricultural advancements exemplify this dynamism. The heavy plow, with its wheeled frame and moldboard for turning heavy clay soils, appeared in Frankish territories by the 7th century, enabling cultivation of northern Europe's marginal lands and contributing to surplus production.[](https://interestingengineering.com/lists/18-inventions-of-the-middle-ages-that-changed-the-world) Complementing this, the three-field crop rotation system, documented from the 8th century onward, replaced the less efficient two-field method, allowing more land under cultivation and yield increases estimated at up to 50% in some regions.[](https://www.thoughtco.com/middle-ages-timeline-1992478) These innovations underpinned demographic expansion: Europe's population grew from approximately 35 million around 1000 CE to 80 million by 1340, reflecting sustained food security absent in a truly backward society.[](https://courses.lumenlearning.com/atd-herkimer-westerncivilization/chapter/daily-medieval-life/)\n\nHydraulic engineering further demonstrates practical ingenuity. By 1086, the Domesday Book recorded over 5,600 mills—mostly water-powered—in England alone, harnessing rivers for grinding grain at scales surpassing late Roman capacities in the region.[](https://www.discovermagazine.com/science-was-alive-and-well-in-the-dark-ages-42197) Windmills, first attested in England in the 1180s, extended this mechanization to areas lacking reliable water flow, powering not only milling but later also drainage and forging.[](https://historyforatheists.com/2024/09/the-great-myths-15-what-about-the-dark-ages/) Such technologies supported urbanization, with towns like 12th-century Paris and London expanding beyond Roman precedents, fostering trade networks evidenced by the revival of fairs in Champagne from the 12th century.[](https://www.medievalists.net/2023/06/medieval-inventions-world/)\n\nIntellectual and structural achievements refute notions of universal superstition stifling inquiry. Universities emerged as organized centers of learning, with Bologna founded in 1088 for law, followed by Paris (c. 1150) and Oxford (c. 1167), where curricula preserved and expanded Roman and Aristotelian texts through Scholastic methods.[](https://www.discovermagazine.com/science-was-alive-and-well-in-the-dark-ages-42197) Gothic architecture, pioneered in the Île-de-France region from the 1130s, employed pointed arches, ribbed vaults, and flying buttresses to erect cathedrals like Notre-Dame de Paris (1163–1345), structures taller and lighter than Roman basilicas, requiring precise geometric knowledge and load-bearing calculations.[](https://going-medieval.com/2017/05/26/theres-no-such-thing-as-the-dark-ages-but-ok/) These feats, sustained over centuries without modern tools, underscore engineering sophistication often dismissed in favor of anachronistic comparisons to antiquity or the Renaissance.[](https://irpathak.medium.com/4-stupid-stories-about-the-middle-ages-everyone-tells-1cb348d094cf)\n\nPersistent myths of medieval backwardness often stem from sources biased toward classical revivalism or modern exceptionalism, such as 19th-century Whig historians who projected linear progress narratives, yet empirical reassessments—incorporating mill counts, crop yield proxies from pollen analysis, and manuscript survivals—reveal a period of adaptive resilience rather than wholesale regression.[](https://historyforatheists.com/2024/09/the-great-myths-15-what-about-the-dark-ages/) While challenges like feudal fragmentation existed, the era's contributions in mechanized production, legal codification (e.g., Magna Carta 1215), and proto-scientific optics studies by figures like Robert Grosseteste (c. 1175–1253) affirm a trajectory of causal progress driven by necessity and empirical trial, not obscured by dogmatic inertia as popularly alleged.[](https://www.quora.com/Why-did-science-make-little-real-progress-in-Europe-in-the-Middle-Ages-3)\n\n### Modern Revisions and Empirical Reassessments\n\nSince the mid-20th century, empirical historiography has dismantled the Enlightenment-era depiction of the Middle Ages as uniformly regressive, employing archaeological data, fiscal records, and paleodemographic analysis to reveal phases of demographic expansion, technological adaptation, and institutional maturation. Disruptions following the Western Roman Empire's collapse in 476 AD were real but regionally varied, with Carolingian reforms under Charlemagne (r. 768–814) fostering scriptoria that preserved Latin texts and initiated administrative standardization, as evidenced by over 7,000 surviving manuscripts from the period. Modern reassessments, informed by dendrochronology and pollen analysis, attribute recovery to climatic amelioration during the Medieval Warm Period (c. 950–1250), which extended growing seasons and supported surplus agriculture, rather than attributing stagnation to ideological factors like Christianity, a claim rooted in unsubstantiated 18th-century polemics.[](https://historyforatheists.com/2024/09/the-great-myths-15-what-about-the-dark-ages/)\n\nQuantitative reconstructions of population dynamics, drawn from Domesday Book surveys (1086) and papal tithe records, indicate Europe's inhabitants roughly doubled from 38–40 million in 1000 AD to 70–75 million by 1300, reflecting caloric surpluses from innovations such as the rigid horse collar (adopted c. 9th–10th centuries) and three-field rotation, which increased arable output by 30–50% in northern soils. Urbanization accompanied this, with over 100 towns exceeding 10,000 residents by 1300, corroborated by excavation yields of imported ceramics and coins signaling transregional commerce. The Commercial Revolution (c. 950–1350) amplified this through credit instruments like bills of exchange, pioneered by Genoese and Florentine bankers, enabling trade volumes in spices and textiles that generated annual fair revenues in Champagne exceeding 100,000 pounds of silver by the 12th century. These patterns, quantified via merchant account books, refute overreliance on anecdotal chronicles prone to elite biases, highlighting instead market-driven efficiencies.[](https://sourcebooks.web.fordham.edu/source/europe-populationetstimates500-1500.asp)[](https://www.economics.utoronto.ca/munro5/L02MedievalPopulationC.pdf)[](https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/commercial-revolution-of-the-middle-ages-9501350/27C2AF7F2C913BADCDC29631B71EA7BF)\n\nTechnological momentum is empirically traced through patent-like guild regulations and artifact typologies: windmills proliferated post-1180 for drainage and milling, mechanical clocks emerged c. 1270–1300 in monasteries for precise horology, and convex lenses for vision correction appeared in northern Italy by 1286, as documented in friar manuscripts. Intellectual reassessments credit the 12th-century translation movement—importing over 400 Arabic-compiled Greek works via Toledo—with fueling proto-empirical methods, evident in Robert Grosseteste's (c. 1175–1253) optical experiments using controlled variables, precursors to modern science. Such findings, bolstered by Bryan Ward-Perkins' analysis of fineware distribution showing no absolute decline in production post-500 AD, counter academic traditions that minimized medieval agency to exalt Renaissance novelty, often reflecting 19th-century nationalist agendas. Causal analysis prioritizes resource constraints and diffusion lags over cultural pathologies, affirming the era's role in foundational Western advancements like representative assemblies (e.g., English Parliament 1265) and double-entry bookkeeping (c. 1340).[](https://www.medievalists.net/2023/06/medieval-inventions-world/)[](https://historyforatheists.com/2024/09/the-great-myths-15-what-about-the-dark-ages/)[](https://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/the-myth-of-the-dark-ages-ignores-how-classical-traditions-flourished-around-the-world-180982190/)"])</script><script nonce="YjU4NmQyOTUtZjdmZS00N2I5LWI3OTYtOGRhNmNlNGE0YWFj">self.__next_f.push([1,"8:[\"$\",\"$L1f\",null,{\"state\":{\"mutations\":[],\"queries\":[{\"dehydratedAt\":1761634667029,\"state\":{\"data\":{\"page\":{\"citations\":[{\"id\":\"1\",\"title\":\"The Legacy of the Middle Ages in the Renaissance and Beyond\",\"description\":\"May 13, 2016 · Today, the period in Europe from about the year 500 through approximately 1500 CE is called the Middle Ages, or the medieval era (the word ...\",\"url\":\"https://dcc.newberry.org/?p=14383\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"2\",\"title\":\"The Middle Ages Contributions to Cardiovascular Medicine - PMC\",\"description\":\"The historical period called the Middle Ages, a long interval between the 5th and the 15th centuries, is still commonly known as the Dark Ages, ...\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5062728/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"3\",\"title\":\"The Middle Ages\",\"description\":\"Most of Europe was decentralized, rural, parochial. Life was reduced to the “laws of nature:” The powerful ruled, while the powerless looked only to survive.Missing:  key | Show results with:key\",\"url\":\"http://webspace.ship.edu/cgboer/middleages.html\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"4\",\"title\":\"Reading: Early Medieval Europe – Birth of Europe\",\"description\":\"Early medieval Europe was defined by shared cultural traits, above all having to do with religion.\",\"url\":\"https://pressbooks.cuny.edu/thebirthofeurope/chapter/chapter-13-early-medieval-europe/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"5\",\"title\":\"The High Middle Ages - Yale University Press\",\"description\":\"Aug 18, 2020 · Self-discovery and definition marked the high Middle Ages (1000–1300). In this period Western people began to assert their identity as they came to know and ...\",\"url\":\"https://yalebooks.yale.edu/2020/08/18/the-high-middle-ages/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"6\",\"title\":\"Thinking about the Dark Ages - The Historians' Sketchpad\",\"description\":\"Oct 14, 2021 · When we talk about the 'Dark Ages' we make it harder for us to understand our past and thus claim the historical inheritance that belongs to all of us.\",\"url\":\"https://salutemmundo.wordpress.com/2021/10/14/thinking-about-the-dark-ages/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"7\",\"title\":\"[PDF] The Dark and Middle Ages - ScholarWorks at WMU\",\"description\":\"Dec 14, 2018 · Today's concept of “Dark Ages” as a period of sustained ignorance continues to be relevant to the early history of western civilization. As.\",\"url\":\"https://scholarworks.wmich.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1021\u0026context=english_pubs\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"8\",\"title\":\"Why the Middle Ages are called the 'Dark Ages' - Medievalists.net\",\"description\":\"Jun 13, 2023 · It was a metaphor used by the 14th-century Italian poet Petrarch, who was a great admirer of the ancient Romans and Greeks.\",\"url\":\"https://www.medievalists.net/2023/06/middle-ages-dark-ages/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"9\",\"title\":\"How and when did the medieval period get its name? - HistoryExtra\",\"description\":\"Nov 1, 2016 · In European history, the Middle Ages or the medieval period ... Medium Aevum”. Ad. This article was taken from BBC History Revealed ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.historyextra.com/period/medieval/how-when-why-did-we-start-calling-medieval-era-name/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"10\",\"title\":\"Why Was 900 Years of European History Called 'the Dark Ages'?\",\"description\":\"They saw the Middle Ages as 'dark' for both its lack of records, and the central role of organised religion, contrasting against the lighter periods of ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.historyhit.com/why-were-the-early-middle-ages-called-the-dark-ages/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"11\",\"title\":\"Flavio Biondo | Renaissance Italy, Humanism, Architectural Historian\",\"description\":\"Flavio Biondo was a humanist historian of the Renaissance and author of the first history ... period of the Middle Ages. The work was consulted by Niccolò ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.britannica.com/biography/Flavio-Biondo\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"12\",\"title\":\"Flavio Biondo – historian and archaeologist | Italy On This Day\",\"description\":\"Jun 4, 2020 · Flavio Biondo, the first historian to write about the concept of the Middle Ages, died on this day in 1463 in Rome.\",\"url\":\"https://www.italyonthisday.com/2020/06/flavio-biondo-15th-century-historian-archaeologist.html\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"13\",\"title\":\"History of Europe - Medieval, Feudalism, Crusades | Britannica\",\"description\":\"Sep 10, 2025 · ... history extending from about 500 to 1400–1500 ce is traditionally known as the Middle Ages. The term was first used by 15th-century scholars ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.britannica.com/topic/history-of-Europe/The-Middle-Ages\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"14\",\"title\":\"Where did the term \\\"Middle Ages\\\" come from?\",\"description\":\"Oct 12, 2011 · It was a loan translation (calque) from Latin medium aevum, attested from the 1610s. The Latin term itself was first recorded in 1604, and ...Missing:  coined | Show results with:coined\",\"url\":\"https://history.stackexchange.com/questions/90/where-did-the-term-middle-ages-come-from\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"15\",\"title\":\"Origin and Definition of the Term \\\"Medieval\\\" - ThoughtCo\",\"description\":\"May 12, 2025 · The idea of the middle ages has been around for centuries, but the word \\\"medieval\\\" wasn't used until the 19th century.\",\"url\":\"https://www.thoughtco.com/definition-of-medieval-1789185\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"16\",\"title\":\"The Middle Ages in modern historiography - Britannica\",\"description\":\"Sep 10, 2025 · Middle Ages remains both a commonplace colloquial term and the name of a subject of academic study. But the history of the term and the current ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.britannica.com/topic/history-of-Europe/The-Middle-Ages-in-modern-historiography\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"17\",\"title\":\"Roman Emperors - DIR Romulus Augustulus\",\"description\":\"Aug 26, 1997 · Romulus had lost his throne after just over ten months of rule -- his deposition would have occurred on or soon after 4 September 476. One ...\",\"url\":\"https://roman-emperors.sites.luc.edu/auggiero.htm\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"18\",\"title\":\"Romulus Augustulus - Roman Emperor | UNRV Roman History\",\"description\":\"The deposition of Romulus Augustulus in 476 AD has long been taught in Western historiography as a major dividing line between antiquity and the Middle Ages.\",\"url\":\"https://www.unrv.com/emperors/romulus-augustulus.php\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"19\",\"title\":\"September 4, 476 the fall of Romulus Augustulus\",\"description\":\"On September 4, 476, Odoacer deposed the teenage emperor Romulus Augustulus in Ravenna, a hinge moment long treated as the end of the Western Roman Empire.\",\"url\":\"https://www.americanstandardgold.com/blog/the-day-the-western-roman-empire-ended.cfm\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"20\",\"title\":\"The Fall of Constantinople · Theodosian Walls\",\"description\":\"The infamous date of the Fall of Constantinople is May 29, 1453 after the siege that began on April 6.\",\"url\":\"https://hum54-15.omeka.fas.harvard.edu/exhibits/show/theodosian_walls_gardens/fall\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"21\",\"title\":\"The Siege of Constantinople (1453) - Medievalists.net\",\"description\":\"Apr 16, 2024 · One of the key dates from the Middle Ages is the year 1453. In this episode of Bow \u0026 Blade, Michael and Kelly discuss how the Ottomans were ...\u003c|separator|\u003e\",\"url\":\"https://www.medievalists.net/2024/04/the-siege-of-constantinople-1453/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"22\",\"title\":\"Middle Ages: Definition and Timeline | HISTORY\",\"description\":\"May 30, 2012 · People use the phrase “Middle Ages” to describe Europe between the fall of Rome in 476 CE and the beginning of the Renaissance in the 14th ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.history.com/topics/middle-ages\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"23\",\"title\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"url\":\"https://www.smarthistory.org/introduction-to-the-middle-ages/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"24\",\"title\":\"[PDF] The End of the Middle Ages and the Problem of Periodization\",\"description\":\"Apr 13, 2023 · The Earliest sources of the fall come from a diverse group of peoples most of whom had either been witnesses to the Siege of Constantinople or ...\",\"url\":\"https://irl.umsl.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1448\u0026context=thesis\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"25\",\"title\":\"Medieval, the Middle Ages - The University of Chicago Press: Journals\",\"description\":\"There is an added difficulty of periodization in that the Middle Ages last ... If classicists and Renaissance scholars don't know when their periods begin and end ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/pdf/10.2307/2846695\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"26\",\"title\":\"[PDF] The History of Florence in the Late Middle Ages and the Renaissance\",\"description\":\"This article will first discuss the ways in which previous scholars attempted to draw chronological boundaries between the Middle Ages and the Renaissance and ...\",\"url\":\"https://ejournals.bc.edu/index.php/orac/article/download/13673/10847/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"27\",\"title\":\"When Were the \\\"Middle Ages\\\"? Periodization and Why it Matters\",\"description\":\"Apr 10, 2023 · Below, I'm going to talk through the problematics of periodization in general and that of the Middle Ages in particular, suggesting ways to ...Problems with Periodization · So When Were the Middle Ages?\",\"url\":\"https://trmarvin.org/when-were-the-middle-ages-periodization-and-why-it-matters/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"28\",\"title\":\"When were the Middle Ages? - Medievalists.net\",\"description\":\"Aug 28, 2012 · Medieval historians have been debating for many years on when were the Middle Ages – was there a year that medieval period began, ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.medievalists.net/2012/08/when-were-the-middle-ages-2/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"29\",\"title\":\"[PDF] PERIODIZATION AND “THE MEDIEVAL GLOBE”: A CONVERSATION\",\"description\":\"Jun 29, 2016 · I have argued that this periodization is fundamental to dominant modes of sovereignty, and if that is correct, then “the medieval globe” might ...\",\"url\":\"https://scholar.harvard.edu/files/puett/files/davis_puett_medieval_globe.pdf\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"30\",\"title\":\"[PDF] The “Dark Ages” Debate - OER Project\",\"description\":\"1 The Middle Ages was a period in Europe. It started with the fall of Rome in the fifth century. It ended with the start of the Renaissance in the.\u003c|separator|\u003e\",\"url\":\"https://www.oerproject.com/-/media/WHP/PDF/Era4/WHP-4-3-2-Read---The-Dark-Ages-Debate---610L.pdf\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"31\",\"title\":\"Pieces of History Jacques Le Goff on Historical Periodization\",\"description\":\"Nov 20, 2014 · In his last published essay, Jacques Le Goff, who recently passed away, examines the problem of historical periodization.\",\"url\":\"https://laviedesidees.fr/Pieces-of-History\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"32\",\"title\":\"Full article: Periodisation and modernity: an introduction\",\"description\":\"Jul 23, 2024 · This issue aims to critically reevaluate classical periodisation models and explore alternative paradigms.\",\"url\":\"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/01916599.2024.2373542\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"33\",\"title\":\"1.1 Periodization and historiography of the Middle Ages - Fiveable\",\"description\":\"Studying this period presents unique challenges, including limited and biased sources, preservation issues, and the risk of anachronistic interpretation.\",\"url\":\"https://fiveable.me/the-middle-ages/unit-1/periodization-historiography-middle-ages/study-guide/ujv1onxdMXfhsMzZ\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"34\",\"title\":\"Let me explain something to you: periodisation and the Middle Ages\",\"description\":\"Feb 26, 2024 · ... history to grapple with, and a lively debate about how to periodise that. ... Periodization and Temporal Boundaries–Defining the Middle Ages.\",\"url\":\"https://going-medieval.com/2024/02/26/let-me-explain-something-to-you-periodisation-and-the-middle-ages/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"35\",\"title\":\"The Crisis of the Third Century - World History Encyclopedia\",\"description\":\"Nov 9, 2017 · After the assassination of Alexander Severus, the empire would see over 20 emperors rise and fall in the almost 50 years between 235-284 CE as ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.worldhistory.org/Crisis_of_the_Third_Century/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"36\",\"title\":\"Ancient History in depth: Third Century Crisis of the Roman Empire\",\"description\":\"Feb 17, 2011 · Internal law and order broke down. Soldiers bullied and exploited civilians. Foreign peoples invaded Roman provinces, killing and destroying, ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.bbc.co.uk/history/ancient/romans/thirdcenturycrisis_article_01.shtml\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"37\",\"title\":\"HIST 210 - Lecture 2 - The Crisis of the Third Century and the ...\",\"description\":\"Internally, the Empire struggled with the problem of succession, an economy wracked by inflation, and the decline of the local elite which had once held it ...\",\"url\":\"https://oyc.yale.edu/history/hist-210/lecture-2\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"38\",\"title\":\"The Debasement of Roman Coinage During the Third-Century Crisis\",\"description\":\"Jun 19, 2025 · One of the many problems of the Third Century Crisis in the Roman Empire was a severe debasement of the precious metal content in coins, which made them less ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.thecollector.com/inflation-third-century-crisis/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"39\",\"title\":\"Currency and the Collapse of the Roman Empire - The Money Project\",\"description\":\"Feb 18, 2016 · How currency debasement, soaring costs, and overtaxing helped lead to the collapse of Ancient Rome's economy and empire.\",\"url\":\"https://money.visualcapitalist.com/currency-and-the-collapse-of-the-roman-empire/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"40\",\"title\":\"Inflation and the Fall of the Roman Empire - Mises Institute\",\"description\":\"Roman historians refer to this period as the “Crisis of the 3rd Century.” And the reason is that the problems of the Roman society in that period were so ...\",\"url\":\"https://mises.org/mises-daily/inflation-and-fall-roman-empire\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"41\",\"title\":\"Scopes and consequences of global plagues in the Roman Empire ...\",\"description\":\"This article focuses on the impact that the Cyprian plague had in the context of the crisis of the third century, its mortality is estimated between 10-20% of ...\",\"url\":\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33399666/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"42\",\"title\":\"Plague of Cyprian, 250-270 CE - World History Encyclopedia\",\"description\":\"Dec 13, 2016 · The disease episode of the mid-200s CE caused political, military, economic and religious upheaval. In addition to the thousands of people dying ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.worldhistory.org/article/992/plague-of-cyprian-250-270-ce/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"43\",\"title\":\"The “Plague of Cyprian”: A revised view of the origin and spread of a ...\",\"description\":\"Jun 7, 2021 · It contends that the disease entered the Roman Empire via Gothic invasions on the Danube rather than traveling up the Nile from inner Africa. It ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-roman-archaeology/article/plague-of-cyprian-a-revised-view-of-the-origin-and-spread-of-a-3rdc-ce-pandemic/4DD870D473D3DDB1DF0328CE2C7E3280\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"44\",\"title\":\"The Plague of Cyprian: Disease, Faith, and Crisis in the Roman ...\",\"description\":\"Sep 11, 2025 · The Plague of Cyprian struck at the empire's armies, emptied its cities, and unsettled its faith in the old gods.\",\"url\":\"https://romanempiretimes.com/the-plague-of-cyprian-disease-faith-and-crisis-in-the-roman-empire/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"45\",\"title\":\"8 Reasons Why Rome Fell - History.com\",\"description\":\"Jan 14, 2014 · A further blow came in the fifth century, when the Vandals claimed North Africa and began disrupting the empire's trade by prowling the ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.history.com/articles/8-reasons-why-rome-fell\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"46\",\"title\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"url\":\"https://www.historyskills.com/classroom/ancient-history/crisis-of-the-third-century/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"47\",\"title\":\"Roman Currency Debasement - UNRV.com\",\"description\":\"Roman Currency Debasement. Rome's Monetary Bedrock. The Roman Empire's ... Nonetheless, because older debased coins remained in circulation - and the empire ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.unrv.com/economy/currency-debasement.php\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"48\",\"title\":\"[PDF] Roman Empire: The Crisis of the 3rd Century AD Contributory Factors\",\"description\":\"The Civil Wars: Civil wars followed the assassination of the emperor Commodus in AD 192. Their effects on the empire were far worse than those of the plague.\",\"url\":\"https://dacollege.org/uploads/stdmat/history-sem2-cc4-Roman-Empire%2520_2.pdf\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"49\",\"title\":\"What were the Barbarian Invasions? - World History Edu\",\"description\":\"Apr 2, 2025 · Also known as the Barbarian Invasions, the Migration Period began mainly due to the arrival of the Huns in Eastern Europe around 375 AD.\",\"url\":\"https://worldhistoryedu.com/what-were-the-barbarian-invasions/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"50\",\"title\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"url\":\"https://www.historyskills.com/classroom/year-8/yr-8-barbarian-migrations-reading/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"51\",\"title\":\"Migration Period | History, Timeline \u0026 Events - Study.com\",\"description\":\"In the early 400s, the Gothic king Alaric invaded Italy and sacked Rome in 410 CE. What did the barbarian invasions do to Rome? The so-called barbarian ...\",\"url\":\"https://study.com/academy/lesson/migration-period-overview-history-barbarian-invasions.html\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"52\",\"title\":\"Who were the Vandals, the 'barbarians' who sacked Rome?\",\"description\":\"Sep 2, 2022 · The Vandals were a Germanic people who sacked Rome and founded a kingdom in North Africa that flourished for about a century, until it was conquered by the ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.livescience.com/who-were-the-vandals\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"53\",\"title\":\"The Visigoths in Spain \u0026 Rome | Overview, History \u0026 Culture\",\"description\":\"The Visigoth kingdom disappeared in Spain, but it left behind successor states, united in their shared Visigoth-Roman culture and Catholicism, which would ...Missing:  Hispania | Show results with:Hispania\",\"url\":\"https://study.com/academy/lesson/the-visigoths-in-spain-rome.html\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"54\",\"title\":\"Theoderic the Great | Western Civilization - Lumen Learning\",\"description\":\"Theoderic the Great (454–526) was king of the Ostrogoths (475–526), ruler of Italy (493–526), regent of the Visigoths (511–526), and a patricius of the Roman ...\",\"url\":\"https://courses.lumenlearning.com/atd-herkimer-westerncivilization/chapter/theoderic-the-great/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"55\",\"title\":\"How Clovis Created a Basis for French National Identity in the 5th ...\",\"description\":\"After crushing the last remnant of the Western Roman Empire, Clovis achieved religious unity across his lands by converting his people to Christianity.Missing:  unification | Show results with:unification\u003c|separator|\u003e\",\"url\":\"https://www.historyhit.com/how-clovis-created-a-basis-for-french-national-identity-in-the-5th-and-6th-centuries/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"56\",\"title\":\"Barbarigenesis and the collapse of complex societies: Rome and after\",\"description\":\"Sep 16, 2021 · ... Roman empire in the West was divided among a handful of successor states ruled by Germanic elites. Consistent with Olson's analysis, the new ...\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8445445/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"57\",\"title\":\"1320: Section 8: The Fall of Rome: Facts and Fictions\",\"description\":\"Germanic tribes seized the opportunity to occupy Britain, particularly the Angles and the Saxons. Leaks were fast becoming floods. His mind poisoned by court ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.usu.edu/markdamen/1320hist\u0026civ/chapters/08romfal.htm\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"58\",\"title\":\"Roman-Byzantine Continuity of Empire\",\"description\":\"The “Byzantine” Empire is the direct continuation of the Eastern Roman Empire in the medieval period, lasting beyond the fall of the Western Roman Empire.\",\"url\":\"https://shadowsofconstantinople.com/roman-continuity/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"59\",\"title\":\"Collections: Rome: Decline and Fall? Part II: Institutions\",\"description\":\"Jan 28, 2022 · The Eastern Roman Empire was more urbanized, more densely peopled and wealthier than the West, but it also faced arguably more severe security ...\",\"url\":\"https://acoup.blog/2022/01/28/collections-rome-decline-and-fall-part-ii-institutions/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"60\",\"title\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"url\":\"https://historyguild.org/byzantium-the-new-rome/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"61\",\"title\":\"The Byzantine State under Justinian I (Justinian the Great)\",\"description\":\"Apr 1, 2009 · In foreign policy, Justinian sought to recover regions lost to foreign invaders, particularly Germanic tribes in Italy and North Africa. He thus ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/the-byzantine-state-under-justinian-i-justinian-the-great\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"62\",\"title\":\"Justinian I's Surprising Recapture of Rome: What Happened?\",\"description\":\"Apr 7, 2025 · The Byzantine Emperor Justinian I came close to uniting the former Roman Empire less than a century after its fall. However, popular discontent, ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.thecollector.com/justinian-i-recapture-rome/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"63\",\"title\":\"How Did the Byzantine Empire Last So Long? - Tales of Byzantium\",\"description\":\"Dec 20, 2019 · The Byzantine Empire lasted long due to a strong economy, competent military, great location, and its ability to adapt and rebuild after the ...\",\"url\":\"http://byzantium.filminspector.com/2019/12/how-did-byzantine-empire-last-so-long.html\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"64\",\"title\":\"Byzantine diplomacy: The elixir of longevity - Diplo Foundation\",\"description\":\"The empire survived through adaptation, and its backbone was its administration. It managed to adapt to the frequent changes of rulers and continuous crises on ...Missing:  antiquity | Show results with:antiquity\",\"url\":\"https://www.diplomacy.edu/histories/byzantine-diplomacy-the-elixir-of-longevity/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"65\",\"title\":\"Guided practice: continuity and change in the Byzantine Empire\",\"description\":\"The first truly strong Byzantine Emperor was Justinian—who ruled the Byzantine Empire from 527 CE to 565 CE. He was able to reclaim much of the Western Empire ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.khanacademy.org/humanities/world-history/medieval-times/byzantine-empire/a/the-rise-of-the-byzantine-empire\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"66\",\"title\":\"The Justinianic Plague: An inconsequential pandemic? - PMC\",\"description\":\"Dec 2, 2019 · The Justinianic Plague (circa 541 to 750 CE) caused tens of millions of deaths throughout the Mediterranean world and Europe, helping to end antiquity and ...\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6926030/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"67\",\"title\":\"The Justinianic Plague | Origins\",\"description\":\"Jun 12, 2020 · They argued that the plague was a global, catastrophic event, which killed millions of inhabitants, paralyzed the economy, stymied urban ...\",\"url\":\"https://origins.osu.edu/connecting-history/covid-justinianic-plague-lessons\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"68\",\"title\":\"The Lombard Destruction of Italy (6th century)\",\"description\":\"In the 6th century there were constant bouts of plague, famine, raids, and fracturing of Italy into warring Lombard and Roman regions. This broke down the trade ...Missing:  facts | Show results with:facts\",\"url\":\"https://shadowsofconstantinople.com/the-lombard-destruction-of-roman-italy/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"69\",\"title\":\"How and why did Rome experience such massive depopulation ...\",\"description\":\"Aug 6, 2022 · The major population drop was during the wars of Justinian in the 6th century. The Eastern Romans attempted to reconquer Rome from the Goths and ...\u003c|separator|\u003e\",\"url\":\"https://www.quora.com/How-and-why-did-Rome-experience-such-massive-depopulation-after-the-Western-Empires-collapse-from-1-million-people-to-only-50-000-in-the-Middle-Ages\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"70\",\"title\":\"[PDF] Trade and the End of Antiquity - Johannes Boehm\",\"description\":\"Abstract. What caused the end of antiquity, the shift of economic activity away from the Mediterranean towards northern Europe and the Middle East?\",\"url\":\"https://jmboehm.github.io/coins.pdf\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"71\",\"title\":\"The Balanced Economic Growth of Carolingian Europe - jstor\",\"description\":\"Changes in urban population levels during the Carolingian period are more difficult to discern. A great enlargement of population on a twelfth century scale ...Missing:  localism | Show results with:localism\",\"url\":\"https://www.jstor.org/stable/202330\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"72\",\"title\":\"(PDF) Early European towns. The development of the economy in ...\",\"description\":\"The development of the economy in the Frankish realm between dynamism and deceleration AD 500-1100.\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/355859623_Early_European_towns_The_development_of_the_economy_in_the_Frankish_realm_between_dynamism_and_deceleration_AD_500-1100\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"73\",\"title\":\"Rashidun | History, Caliphs, \u0026 Facts - Britannica\",\"description\":\"The caliphate of the Rashidun, in which virtually all actions had religious import, began with the wars of the riddah (“apostasy”; 632–633), tribal uprisings ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Rashidun\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"74\",\"title\":\"11.2 The Arab-Islamic Conquests and the First Islamic States\",\"description\":\"Apr 19, 2023 · Nonetheless, starting in 634 and continuing into the early eighth century, they found enormous success conquering much of the territory around ...Missing:  timeline 8th\",\"url\":\"https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-1/pages/11-2-the-arab-islamic-conquests-and-the-first-islamic-states\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"75\",\"title\":\"The Arab conquests 634-751 - The map as history\",\"description\":\"The Arab conquests (634-751) saw initial victories, the collapse of the Persian Empire, and the conquest of Syria, Palestine, Egypt, and the Persian territory. ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.the-map-as-history.com/origins-of-islam-and-the-arabo-muslim-empire/the-arab-conquests-634-751\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"76\",\"title\":\"Map of the Islamic Conquests in the 7th-9th Centuries\",\"description\":\"Jun 8, 2021 · Beginning in Arabia, the new faith and its armies spread under the Rashidun Caliphs (632–661 CE), followed by the Umayyad Caliphate (661–750 CE) ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.worldhistory.org/image/14212/map-of-the-islamic-conquests-in-the-7th-9th-centur/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"77\",\"title\":\"The Umayyad Caliphate Invasion of Spain - Medieval History\",\"description\":\"Feb 21, 2023 · Tariq ibn Ziyad and the Battle of Guadalete. During the reign of al-Walid I, Tariq ibn Ziyad led Berber forces in a successful invasion of ...\",\"url\":\"https://historymedieval.com/the-umayyad-caliphate-invasion-of-spain/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"78\",\"title\":\"Battle of Tours | Facts, History, \u0026 Importance - Britannica\",\"description\":\"Battle of Tours, (October 732), victory won by Charles Martel, the de facto ruler of the Frankish kingdoms, over Muslim invaders from Spain.\",\"url\":\"https://www.britannica.com/event/Battle-of-Tours-732\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"79\",\"title\":\"8.3: The Rise of Islam, Carolingian Europe, New Kingdoms, and a ...\",\"description\":\"Jan 14, 2021 · Christianity was a major unifying factor between Eastern and western Europe before the Arab conquests, but the conquest of North Africa sundered ...\",\"url\":\"https://human.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Humanities/Humanities%253A_Prehistory_to_the_15th_Century_%28Lumen%29/08%253A_Western_European_Civilization_During_the_Middle_Ages/8.03%253A_The_Rise_of_Islam_Carolingian_Europe_New_Kingdoms_and_a_Revived_Byzantium\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"80\",\"title\":\"The rise of Islamic empires and states (article) - Khan Academy\",\"description\":\"Most of the significant expansion occurred during the reign of the Rashidun from 632 to 661 CE, which was the reign of the first four successors of Muhammad.\",\"url\":\"https://www.khanacademy.org/humanities/world-history/medieval-times/spread-of-islam/a/the-rise-of-islamic-empires-and-states\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"81\",\"title\":\"The Arab Conquest | Silk Roads Programme - UNESCO\",\"description\":\"From the mid-seventh century, Muslim Arab armies from Saudi Arabia began to travel north into Central Asia and west across Africa, invading the countries ...Missing:  timeline | Show results with:timeline\",\"url\":\"https://en.unesco.org/silkroad/knowledge-bank/arab-conquest\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"82\",\"title\":\"Charlemagne and the Carolingian Dynasty | The Middle Ages Class ...\",\"description\":\"Key Dates and Events to Remember · 742 or 747 AD: Birth of Charlemagne · 751 AD: Pepin the Short becomes the first Carolingian king of the Franks · 768 AD: ...\",\"url\":\"https:/