 · Ethics\",\"url\":\"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aquinas/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"163\",\"title\":\"Bologna and the Rise of Medieval Universities - Historiana\",\"description\":\"The first of the medieval universities was the University of Bologna, founded in 1088, although its 'official' charter from Emperor Frederick Barbarossa came ...\",\"url\":\"https://historiana.eu/historical-content/source-collections/bologna-and-the-rise-of-medieval-universities\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"164\",\"title\":\"Medieval and early modern universities\",\"description\":\"The earliest foundations were Bologna, Paris and Oxford but these were followed by dozens more in the next few centuries.\",\"url\":\"https://www.hps.cam.ac.uk/students/research-guide/medieval-early-modern-universities\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"165\",\"title\":\"10 of the oldest universities in the world | TopUniversities\",\"description\":\"Aug 21, 2024 · The University of Bologna (1088), Oxford (1096-1167), and Al-Azhar University (970 AD) are among the oldest universities. Most are in Europe, ...University of Bologna · University of Oxford · University of Paris · University of Padua\",\"url\":\"https://www.topuniversities.com/blog/10-oldest-universities-world\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"166\",\"title\":\"What impact did Scholasticism have on church history?\",\"description\":\"Aug 9, 2022 · The crowning achievement of Scholasticism was the work of Thomas Aquinas (c. 1224–1274) and his masterful attempt to reconcile faith and reason.\",\"url\":\"https://www.gotquestions.org/Scholasticism.html\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"167\",\"title\":\"The history of pre-modern European universities in a nutshell - CEPR\",\"description\":\"Jul 24, 2024 · During the first period (1000–1199), Paris and Bologna emerge as the two most central universities, a result that aligns with expectations.\",\"url\":\"https://cepr.org/voxeu/columns/history-pre-modern-european-universities-nutshell\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"168\",\"title\":\"Birth of the Gothic: Abbot Suger and the ambulatory at St. Denis\",\"description\":\"Artwork details ; Dates, 1140–44 ; Places, Europe / Western Europe / France ; Period, Culture, Style, Medieval / Gothic ; Artwork Type, Architecture / Church.\",\"url\":\"https://smarthistory.org/birth-of-the-gothic-abbot-suger-and-the-ambulatory-at-st-denis/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"169\",\"title\":\"Basilica of Saint-Denis: The Cradle of Gothic Architecture\",\"description\":\"Feb 2, 2021 · The Basilica of Saint-Denis is the earliest known example of Gothic architecture and provided a model for ecclesiastical structures throughout the Middle Ages.\",\"url\":\"https://www.thecollector.com/gothic-architecture-saint-denis-basilica/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"170\",\"title\":\"Gothic architecture, an introduction - Smarthistory\",\"description\":\"The pointed arch was a Gothic innovation that allowed Gothic architects to do what they really wanted to do, which was to build larger and brighter churches.\",\"url\":\"https://smarthistory.org/gothic-architecture-an-introduction/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"171\",\"title\":\"Gothic cathedrals | History of Architecture Class Notes - Fiveable\",\"description\":\"Gothic cathedrals revolutionized medieval architecture, emerging in the 12th century with innovative features like pointed arches and ribbed vaults.\",\"url\":\"https://fiveable.me/history-of-architecture/unit-4/gothic-cathedrals/study-guide/BiexEtUs4npn5Ybw\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"172\",\"title\":\"Gothic Art - The Metropolitan Museum of Art\",\"description\":\"Oct 1, 2002 · Gothic art, characterized by pointed arches, emerged in 12th-century Paris. It used stone ribs and flying buttresses, and its style spread ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/gothic-art\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"173\",\"title\":\"Gothic Art and Architecture Overview | TheArtStory\",\"description\":\"Aug 9, 2018 · Gothic art flourished in Western Europe with monumental sculptures and stained-glass window decorated cathedrals - marked by the pointed ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.theartstory.org/movement/gothic-art-and-architecture/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"174\",\"title\":\"Military technology - Cavalry, Warfare, Tactics - Britannica\",\"description\":\"The beginning of the age of cavalry in Europe is traditionally dated to the destruction of the legions of the Roman emperor Valens by Gothic horsemen.\",\"url\":\"https://www.britannica.com/technology/military-technology/The-age-of-cavalry-c-400-ce-1350\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"175\",\"title\":\"Caste, Skill, and Training: The Evolution of Cohesion in European ...\",\"description\":\"Jul 2, 2014 · If heavy cavalry increasingly dominated the high-medieval military scene, this reflected its flexibility and adaptability as well as its ...\u003c|separator|\u003e\",\"url\":\"https://deremilitari.org/2014/07/caste-skill-and-training-the-evolution-of-cohesion-in-european-armies-from-the-middle-ages-to-the-sixteenth-century/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"176\",\"title\":\"The Knights Templar - Historic UK\",\"description\":\"Jan 12, 2024 · The Knights Templar was a military order founded in 1119 which would become one of the strongest, well-known and lucrative of the orders.\",\"url\":\"https://www.historic-uk.com/HistoryUK/HistoryofEngland/The-Knights-Templar/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"177\",\"title\":\"10 Reasons the Knights Templar Were History's Fiercest Fighters\",\"description\":\"Feb 5, 2019 · They acted as the advance force in a number of battles of the Crusades, including the Battle of Montgisard, when they helped greatly outnumbered ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.history.com/articles/knights-templar-facts-crusades-wealth\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"178\",\"title\":\"The Armies of the Crusades - World History Encyclopedia\",\"description\":\"Nov 15, 2018 · It was a common tactic to engage the enemy, fire off a lethal barrage of arrows and then withdraw as quickly as possible to minimise losses.Article · European Armies · Organisation \u0026 TacticsMissing:  reforms | Show results with:reforms\",\"url\":\"https://www.worldhistory.org/article/1281/the-armies-of-the-crusades/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"179\",\"title\":\"Norman Castles in England\",\"description\":\"Archaeologists believe that in the first 20 years of William's reign, the Normans built as many as 500 motte and bailey castles throughout England. And top of ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.essentially-england.com/norman-castles.html\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"180\",\"title\":\"Military technology - Castles, Fortifications, Defense | Britannica\",\"description\":\"Beginning in the 9th century, European engineers constructed wheeled wooden siege towers, called belfroys. These were fitted with drawbridges, which could be ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.britannica.com/technology/military-technology/The-castle\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"181\",\"title\":\"Krak Des Chevaliers - World History Encyclopedia\",\"description\":\"Sep 7, 2018 · The castle was built according to the latest design technology of the 12th and 13th century CE to maximise its defence. The final Hospitaller ...Missing:  significance | Show results with:significance\",\"url\":\"https://www.worldhistory.org/Krak_Des_Chevaliers/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"182\",\"title\":\"[PDF] The Invention of the Counterweight Trebuchet\",\"description\":\"that the counterweight trebuchet \\\"was introduced into Europe in the twelfth century, ... probably does not signal the date of its introduction. The ...\",\"url\":\"https://uccshes.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2012/09/the-invention-of-the-counterweight-trebuchet.pdf\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"183\",\"title\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"url\":\"https://www.britannica.com/technology/trebuchet\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"184\",\"title\":\"Medieval Warfare: How to Capture a Castle with Siegecraft\",\"description\":\"Jun 12, 2006 · During the Middle Ages, a besieging army needed manpower, siege engines, and, more often than not, patience in order to carry a fortress.Onager And Mangonel · Battering Rams And Bores · Siege Tower Or Belfry\",\"url\":\"https://www.historynet.com/how-to-capture-a-castle-with-siegecraft/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"185\",\"title\":\"Notes on Medieval Population Geography | by Lyman Stone - Medium\",\"description\":\"Jul 5, 2016 · England and wales had 20 by 1300, and England alone was probably more like 22–25. Spain and Portugal had about 35 by 1000 AD. That said, high ...\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/migration-issues/notes-on-medieval-population-geography-fd062449364f\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"186\",\"title\":\"Famine, war, and climate change: From the Middle Ages to ... - CEPR\",\"description\":\"Aug 29, 2022 · This column finds that most preindustrial famines occurred at times of high population pressure on resources and were triggered by extreme weather events.\",\"url\":\"https://cepr.org/voxeu/columns/famine-war-and-climate-change-middle-ages-russia-ukraine-war\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"187\",\"title\":\"The Great Famine (1315–1317) - Climate in Arts and History\",\"description\":\"About 5-12% of the population of northern Europe died from starvation or related disease. ... This famine exacerbated the effects of the Black Death, an outbreak ...Missing:  toll | Show results with:toll\",\"url\":\"https://www.science.smith.edu/climatelit/the-great-famine/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"188\",\"title\":\"The Great Famine 1315-1317 - British Food: A History\",\"description\":\"Sep 9, 2020 · One in twenty died during the famine but for those who survived, insult was added to injury because a “great pestilence” came and devasted a ...Missing:  impact | Show results with:impact\",\"url\":\"https://britishfoodhistory.com/2020/09/09/the-great-famine-1315-1317/\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"189\",\"title\":\"The Bright Side of the Black Death | American Scientist\",\"descrip